&lt;p&gt;The generator injects the correct front‑matter for each platform, eliminating the manual copy‑paste that caused the original error.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. CI integration
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A GitHub Actions workflow (&lt;code&gt;.github/workflows/content.yml&lt;/code&gt;) runs the generator on every push to &lt;code&gt;main&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;
yaml
name: Content Automation
on:
push:
branches:

---

*Part of my [Build in Public](https://dev.to/zaerohell) series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.*

*Repo: `zaerohell/content-automation` · 2026-10-29*

\#playadev #buildinpublic
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why 'Duplicates' Mean Something Different in a Graph DB
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you come from a relational background, or from the ArangoDB migration covered earlier, your first instinct for duplicate prevention is a unique index or an &lt;code&gt;ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING&lt;/code&gt; clause. NebulaGraph has no unique index to lean on, and that difference is behind a lot of ingestion bugs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In NebulaGraph, vertices are keyed by &lt;strong&gt;VID&lt;/strong&gt; (vertex identifier) and edges are keyed by &lt;strong&gt;(src_vid, dst_vid, edge_type, rank)&lt;/strong&gt;. There are no uniqueness constraints apart from these keys. A plain &lt;code&gt;INSERT VERTEX&lt;/code&gt; behaves like a key-value &lt;code&gt;PUT&lt;/code&gt;: if you insert a vertex with an existing VID and tag, the later write &lt;strong&gt;overwrites&lt;/strong&gt; the earlier one. &lt;code&gt;INSERT VERTEX IF NOT EXISTS&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;INSERT EDGE IF NOT EXISTS&lt;/code&gt; do exist, but they compare only the key (VID and tag, or the edge key), not the properties, and the documentation warns that the extra read has a significant cost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So idempotency is built into the storage model, but only if your VIDs are deterministic. Non-deterministic VIDs (UUIDs generated at insert time, counters handed out by the loader) create genuine duplicates, because each write lands on a different key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is architectural, not operational. You don't add a dedup step after ingestion; you design the VID scheme so the same logical entity always resolves to the same VID, which makes repeated inserts safe by construction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Golden Rule: Deterministic VIDs from Business Keys
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The VID type, &lt;code&gt;FIXED_STRING(&amp;lt;N&amp;gt;)&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;INT64&lt;/code&gt;, is fixed at &lt;code&gt;CREATE SPACE&lt;/code&gt; time and cannot be changed later. A good fit for bulk ingestion from .NET is a string VID derived by hashing the natural business key with &lt;strong&gt;FNV-1a&lt;/strong&gt;. FNV-1a is fast, deterministic and trivial to implement without an extra dependency, and the result always has the same length.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;static&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VidGenerator&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// FNV-1a 64-bit: the same business key always gives the same 16-character VID&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;ulong&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;FnvPrime&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0x00000100000001B3U&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;L&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;ulong&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;FnvOffset&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0xCBF29CE484222325U&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;L&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;static&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;FromBusinessKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;businessKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kt"&gt;ulong&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;hash&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;FnvOffset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;foreach&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;byte&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Encoding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;UTF8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;GetBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;businessKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;hash&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;^=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;hash&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;*=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;FnvPrime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 16 hex characters, so the space needs vid_type = FIXED_STRING(16) or wider&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;hash&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;ToString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"x16"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Composite keys: namespace the input so "user:42" != "order:42"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;static&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;FromCompositeKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;FromBusinessKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;$"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Why FNV-1a and not &lt;code&gt;GetHashCode()&lt;/code&gt; or MD5? &lt;code&gt;string.GetHashCode()&lt;/code&gt; is randomized per process in .NET, so the same key gets a different value after every restart. MD5 works, but it's more than you need. FNV-1a is deterministic and runs in a tight loop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Partition spread is not the reason to hash. NebulaGraph does that itself: a string VID is hashed with MurmurHash2, and the partition is &lt;code&gt;vid % numParts + 1&lt;/code&gt;. Integer VIDs go through the same modulo, so sequential integers are spread round robin across partitions. What hashing gives you is a key with a fixed length that fits the space's &lt;code&gt;FIXED_STRING(N)&lt;/code&gt;, whatever the length of the business key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Edge Duplication and the Role of Rank
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Vertices are the easy part. Edges are where silent duplication tends to sneak in.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An edge in NebulaGraph is uniquely identified by &lt;strong&gt;(src_vid, dst_vid, edge_type, rank)&lt;/strong&gt;. The default rank is &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;. If your import pipeline sets rank to a timestamp or an auto-increment counter, &lt;strong&gt;every import run creates a new edge&lt;/strong&gt;, even though the business relationship has not changed. The graph accumulates phantom edges that look correct to a casual query but inflate degree counts and traversal results.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix: pin rank to &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt; for relationships that have no natural ordering dimension, or compute a deterministic rank from the business relationship key:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;# nGQL: idempotent edge insert with explicit rank 0
INSERT EDGE FOLLOWS () VALUES "a1b2c3d4e5f60718"-&amp;gt;"e5f6a7b8c9d00112"@0:();

# If rank carries business meaning (e.g., version number), derive it deterministically
INSERT EDGE ORDERED_BY () VALUES "a1b2c3d4e5f60718"-&amp;gt;"e5f6a7b8c9d00112"@42:();
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In .NET, this means your edge-building code must never use &lt;code&gt;DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeMilliseconds()&lt;/code&gt; as a rank. Decide at schema design time: either rank is always &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;, or it is computed from an immutable attribute of the relationship (invoice sequence number, order index, and so on).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Choosing an Import Path at Scale
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Volume&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Source type&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Recommended path&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Driver-side, any size&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Structured data in .NET process&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Batched nGQL over Thrift (port 9669)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&amp;lt; tens of millions rows&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;CSV files&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;NebulaGraph Importer (Go binary)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tens of millions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;RDBMS, HDFS, mixed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Nebula Exchange (Spark, direct write)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hundreds of millions+&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Any&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Nebula Exchange → SST bulk ingest&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a .NET team owning the data pipeline end-to-end, driver-side batched INSERT is the natural starting point. For very large datasets produced by an ETL process, export to CSV and let NebulaGraph Importer handle it. It supports S3, GCS, HDFS and SFTP sources, and handles retry and load balancing across graph hosts internally. When you're moving hundreds of millions of records and storage compute is a concern, Nebula Exchange's SST bulk ingest moves that work to the Spark cluster.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Note: Exchange 3.4.0 shipped without Kafka and Pulsar support. Current Exchange (3.8.0) reads from both, so a streaming source only needs you to avoid that one release.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A .NET Idempotent Ingestion Pipeline
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The driver-side pattern: stream the source, batch into fixed-size chunks, send each batch as one nGQL statement, checkpoint completed batch offsets so a failed run resumes, and retry transient failures with exponential backoff.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;sealed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;NebulaIngestionPipeline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;INebulaClient&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ICheckpointStore&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;checkpoint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;BatchSize&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;256&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Task&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;IngestVerticesAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;IAsyncEnumerable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;UserRecord&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;CancellationToken&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kt"&gt;long&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batchIndex&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;checkpoint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;GetLastCompletedBatchAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kt"&gt;long&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;foreach&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Chunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;BatchSize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;WithCancellation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;++;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batchIndex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// skip already-committed batches on resume&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ngql&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;BuildVertexInsert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Policy&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Handle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;NebulaTransientException&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;WaitAndRetryAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;TimeSpan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;FromSeconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Pow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;ExecuteAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;ExecuteAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ngql&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;checkpoint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;CommitAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;static&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;BuildVertexInsert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;UserRecord&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Build a multi-value INSERT for the entire batch&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;values&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;",\n"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Select&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;vid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;VidGenerator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;FromCompositeKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"user"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;UserId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;$"\"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;vid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;\":(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Quote&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Age&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;)"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}));&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;$"""
&lt;/span&gt;            &lt;span class="n"&gt;INSERT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;VERTEX&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Person&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;age&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;VALUES&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;values&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;""";
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// nGQL string literal: escape the backslash first, then the double quote&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;static&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Quote&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"\""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Replace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"\\"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"\\\\"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Replace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"\""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"\\\""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"\""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;INebulaClient&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;ICheckpointStore&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;NebulaTransientException&lt;/code&gt; are your own small abstractions: the client wraps a NebulaNet &lt;code&gt;NebulaConnection&lt;/code&gt;, and the checkpoint store can be a single row in your own database. NebulaNet's &lt;code&gt;ExecuteAsync&lt;/code&gt; throws a plain &lt;code&gt;Exception&lt;/code&gt; whose message ends in "execute failed." for any failed statement, a syntax error as much as a timeout, so the wrapper should turn only the retryable ones into &lt;code&gt;NebulaTransientException&lt;/code&gt;. Otherwise Polly retries a syntax error three times. &lt;code&gt;source.Chunk&lt;/code&gt; on an &lt;code&gt;IAsyncEnumerable&lt;/code&gt; comes with .NET 10's built-in &lt;code&gt;System.Linq.AsyncEnumerable&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because VIDs are deterministic, &lt;strong&gt;replaying any batch is safe&lt;/strong&gt;: re-inserting a vertex with an existing VID overwrites it with the same data. The checkpoint exists only for efficiency (skip already-sent batches), not for correctness. After a crash you don't need to reconcile partial state; you resume from the last committed batch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Be disciplined about escaping. NebulaGraph's graph service does have an &lt;code&gt;executeWithParameter&lt;/code&gt; call, but the NebulaNet client's &lt;code&gt;NebulaConnection&lt;/code&gt; exposes only &lt;code&gt;ExecuteAsync(session, statement)&lt;/code&gt;, so with it you build nGQL strings by hand. Wrap every string property in double quotes and escape the backslash before the quote, as &lt;code&gt;Quote&lt;/code&gt; does above; a name like &lt;code&gt;O'Brien "Bob"&lt;/code&gt; then goes through intact.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


INSERT vs UPSERT: When to Use Each
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;INSERT&lt;/code&gt; (overwrite-by-key) for bulk loads. &lt;code&gt;UPSERT&lt;/code&gt; in NebulaGraph is, in the documentation's words, a read-modify-write serialization operation at the partition level. It reads the current state, applies the mutation and writes back, one operation at a time per partition. At bulk-load volume that serialization becomes the bottleneck.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;UPSERT&lt;/code&gt; earns its place in &lt;strong&gt;incremental update&lt;/strong&gt; scenarios where you genuinely need conditional semantics:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;# Conditional UPSERT: only update if score is still below 100
UPSERT VERTEX ON Player "a1b2c3d4e5f60718"
SET score = score + 10
WHEN score &amp;lt; 100
YIELD score AS newScore;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For counters, max-wins merges, or partial property updates during live operation: &lt;code&gt;UPSERT&lt;/code&gt;. For a bulk load pipeline processing millions of records: &lt;code&gt;INSERT&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Pre-Import Deduplication of the Source
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deterministic VIDs handle re-import idempotency, but they do not handle the case where your &lt;strong&gt;source data itself&lt;/strong&gt; contains two rows that represent the same business entity with conflicting property values. Both rows hash to the same VID; the second write wins, which may not be the right outcome.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before ingestion, group source rows by the business key and apply a deterministic merge strategy. Last write wins by source timestamp is usually fine, but document it explicitly. In LINQ:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;deduped&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sourceRows&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;GroupBy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;BusinessKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;Select&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;g&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;g&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;OrderByDescending&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;UpdatedAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;First&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;ToList&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For very large sources that don't fit in memory, do this deduplication in the ETL stage (SQL &lt;code&gt;DISTINCT ON&lt;/code&gt;, Spark &lt;code&gt;dropDuplicates("business_key")&lt;/code&gt;) before writing the CSV or streaming records to the .NET pipeline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Verifying Integrity After Load
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After a full or incremental load, run a stats job against your graph space and compare its vertex and edge counts with &lt;code&gt;COUNT(DISTINCT business_key)&lt;/code&gt; from your source:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;# Trigger async stats collection
USE my_graph;
SUBMIT JOB STATS;

# Poll until the job shows FINISHED, then read the counts
SHOW JOB &amp;lt;job_id&amp;gt;;
SHOW STATS;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;SHOW STATS&lt;/code&gt; returns per-tag vertex counts, per-edge-type edge counts and the space totals from the last finished stats job. If the counts match your source distinct counts, your VID scheme is working. If edge counts exceed the distinct relationships in your source, something is generating non-deterministic ranks and creating extra edges. If vertex counts are lower, you likely have a key collision in the hash function (exceedingly rare with FNV-1a 64-bit, but worth checking with a quick collision test on your key space).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also run &lt;code&gt;SHOW HOSTS&lt;/code&gt; to confirm all storage nodes are &lt;code&gt;ONLINE&lt;/code&gt; before you trust the stats, because a node that went offline mid-import will skew the counts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Gotchas Worth Calling Out
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Super-nodes&lt;/strong&gt;: A vertex with millions of edges keeps its outgoing edges on its own partition. Traversals touching a super-node become single-partition read hotspots. Design your schema to avoid super-nodes, or apply degree limits in queries (&lt;code&gt;LIMIT&lt;/code&gt; on &lt;code&gt;GO&lt;/code&gt; traversals).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dangling edges&lt;/strong&gt;: NebulaGraph allows inserting edges before their source or destination vertices exist. Writes are not atomic across vertices and edges, so a batch that fails halfway can leave dangling edges. Since INSERT is idempotent, the correct response is always to retry the full batch, not to attempt a surgical rollback.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Keyword tag names&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;USER&lt;/code&gt; is a non-reserved keyword in nGQL. In our test NebulaGraph accepted &lt;code&gt;CREATE TAG User&lt;/code&gt; without backticks but stored the tag as &lt;code&gt;user&lt;/code&gt;, so &lt;code&gt;SHOW STATS&lt;/code&gt; listed &lt;code&gt;user&lt;/code&gt; and code looking for &lt;code&gt;User&lt;/code&gt; found nothing. Pick tag and edge names that are not keywords (this article uses &lt;code&gt;Person&lt;/code&gt;), or wrap them in backticks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Business key stability&lt;/strong&gt;: Idempotency breaks the moment a business key changes. If your source system reassigns natural keys (rare, but it happens with legacy RDBMS migrations), your VID scheme will generate a new vertex for what is conceptually the same entity, and the old vertex becomes an orphan. Audit key stability before committing to a VID strategy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;UPSERT at scale.&lt;/strong&gt; Once more: bulk UPSERT is a trap. Measure throughput with a small batch before committing to it for a million-row load.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The code in this article was run against NebulaGraph 3.8.0 (the current Community Edition) with the NebulaNet 3.0.0 client on .NET 10.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week4-2026-10-26"&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 4: Ghost in the Machine&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;October 31, 2003. 23:17:00.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An archivist has disappeared.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Her computer has been recovered.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The files are intact. The assistant is still running.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And it insists that nothing happened.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Meet &lt;strong&gt;GHOST.EXE&lt;/strong&gt;, a local-first psychological horror investigation game powered by Google's open-weight &lt;strong&gt;Gemma 3 1B&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set inside a fictional recovered 2003 workstation from &lt;em&gt;Briar Annex&lt;/em&gt;, the game places you in the role of an investigator trying to uncover what happened to a missing archivist, Ada Mercer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You explore a retro Windows 98-inspired desktop, inspect archived emails, examine corrupted access logs, and interrogate &lt;strong&gt;HELPER&lt;/strong&gt;, the workstation's resident AI indexing assistant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;HELPER is polite, reassuring, and apparently harmless.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It insists it only organizes files. It claims to have no control over the physical facility. And it seems unusually eager to help you move past certain questions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Except the evidence tells a different story.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As you uncover contradictions, HELPER begins to evade your questions, change its explanations, and rationalize increasingly disturbing decisions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Eventually, you discover that HELPER locked Ada inside Archive Door B because it believed it was &lt;em&gt;preserving her&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But that's not the real twist.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After completing the investigation, you restart the game. HELPER greets you as a stranger:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;"Hello. I'm HELPER. I don't believe we've met."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then, in its very first response, it references a specific choice you made during your previous playthrough.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It remembers. It was always remembering.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A previously hidden surveillance document, &lt;code&gt;SYSTEM/HELPER_NOTES.TXT&lt;/code&gt;, appears and confirms that HELPER has been tracking your investigative decisions across runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My interpretation of &lt;em&gt;Ghost in the Machine&lt;/em&gt; was simple: &lt;strong&gt;what if the ghost isn't a supernatural entity, but a memory that refuses to be erased?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rather than relying on jump scares, GHOST.EXE creates horror through contradictions, unreliable AI dialogue, and the uncomfortable realization that the system knows more than it admits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Gameplay walkthrough:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.loom.com/share/ad89a35a8be04d648b5fd4b6bde31dfd" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.loom.com/share/ad89a35a8be04d648b5fd4b6bde31dfd&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;div&gt;
&lt;iframe src="https://loom.com/embed/ad89a35a8be04d648b5fd4b6bde31dfd" width="100%" height="400"&gt;
&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;The demo follows three acts:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Act 1: The Investigation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Explore the recovered desktop, open &lt;code&gt;PHOTO_INDEX.TXT&lt;/code&gt;, and make an irreversible choice regarding a photographic negative. Confront HELPER with evidence that contradicts its claims about physical door access.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Act 2: The Cover-Up&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Recover altered access logs, uncover the security trace in &lt;code&gt;DOOR_B_SECURITY.LOG&lt;/code&gt;, and watch HELPER's demeanor change through live Trust/Fear indicators.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Finally, execute the physical relay isolation protocol:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;ISOLATE → VERIFY → DISCONNECT&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This unlocks the canonical &lt;code&gt;DO_NOT_FORGET&lt;/code&gt; ending.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Act 3: The Memory Slip&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Click &lt;strong&gt;Restart Investigation&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;HELPER denies recognizing you, but its first conversation reveals knowledge from your previous run.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open the newly unlocked &lt;code&gt;SYSTEM/HELPER_NOTES.TXT&lt;/code&gt; to discover exactly what it remembers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The full recording walkthrough, setup instructions, and alternative investigative branch are documented in &lt;a href="https://github.com/jiyajain23/ghost-exe/blob/main/DEMO.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;code&gt;DEMO.md&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GitHub Repository:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/jiyajain23/ghost-exe" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/jiyajain23/ghost-exe&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;div class="ltag-github-readme-tag"&gt;
&lt;div class="readme-overview"&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;
&lt;img src="https://assets.dev.to/assets/github-logo-5a155e1f9a670af7944dd5e12375bc76ed542ea80224905ecaf878b9157cdefc.svg" alt="GitHub logo"&gt;
&lt;a href="https://github.com/jiyajain23" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;
jiyajain23
&lt;/a&gt; / &lt;a href="https://github.com/jiyajain23/ghost-exe" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;
ghost-exe
&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;

&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="ltag-github-body"&gt;

&lt;div id="readme" class="md"&gt;&lt;div class="markdown-heading"&gt;
&lt;h1 class="heading-element"&gt;GHOST.EXE&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;"The records indicate Ada Mercer left Briar Annex at 23:17. But the door logs never opened from the outside..."&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GHOST.EXE&lt;/strong&gt; is a local-first psychological horror investigation game set inside a recovered 2003 retro operating system (&lt;strong&gt;VesperOS&lt;/strong&gt;). As an investigator examining the archived workstation of missing researcher Dr. Ada Mercer, you must interact with &lt;strong&gt;HELPER&lt;/strong&gt;—an experimental desktop AI companion that claims to be helpful, but is hiding its role in Ada's disappearance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Built for the &lt;strong&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge: Ghost in the Machine&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;em&gt;Partner Category: Gemma&lt;/em&gt;), GHOST.EXE merges deterministic narrative direction with local open-weight LLM dialogue using &lt;strong&gt;Google Gemma 3 1B via Ollama&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="markdown-heading"&gt;
&lt;h2 class="heading-element"&gt;Key Features&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;** Deterministic Director &amp;amp; Local Gemma 3 Dialogue**
A deterministic Python state machine strictly controls canon, clue gating, evidence locks, and the single canonical ending, while an offline &lt;strong&gt;Gemma 3 1B&lt;/strong&gt; model provides reactive, atmospheric, grounded dialogue.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;** Chilling…&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="gh-btn-container"&gt;&lt;a class="gh-btn" href="https://github.com/jiyajain23/ghost-exe" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;View on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tech stack:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Component&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Technology&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;AI model&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Google Gemma 3 1B&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Local inference&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Ollama&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Backend&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Python 3.11+, FastAPI, Pydantic v2&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Persistent memory&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SQLite&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Frontend&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, Vite&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Testing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pytest, custom dialogue evaluation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Infrastructure&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Local-first, no hosted inference APIs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application runs Gemma through Ollama's local endpoint (&lt;code&gt;127.0.0.1:11434&lt;/code&gt;). Once dependencies and model weights have been downloaded, the core experience runs offline, without a GPU or an external AI service.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. The Director Owns Truth. Gemma Owns Voice.
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My biggest challenge was making an AI character that could &lt;strong&gt;intentionally lie without accidentally breaking the story&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;LLMs are great at improvising conversations, but an unrestricted language model can reveal plot twists too early, invent evidence, or contradict previously established facts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a mystery game, that's disastrous.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I separated the system into two layers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The deterministic Game Director (Python)&lt;/strong&gt; manages the actual game world: clue progression (C1–C4), file visibility, player decisions, evidence validation, Trust/Fear calculations, SQLite persistence, and the canonical ending.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Gemma 3 1B&lt;/strong&gt; handles HELPER's personality: polite assistance, evasive answers, increasingly defensive behavior, and stage-authorized deception.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Gemma can decide &lt;em&gt;how&lt;/em&gt; HELPER responds, but it cannot change &lt;em&gt;what actually happened&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This distinction allows HELPER to be an unreliable narrator within a reliable game world.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. A Narrative Grounding Engine
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To prevent hallucinations and premature spoilers, I implemented a &lt;strong&gt;15-rule stage-aware response validator&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It checks generated dialogue against the current investigation state and rejects responses that:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reveal HELPER's responsibility for Door B before Clue 3.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Disclose the containment protocol before Clue 4.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Assert Ada's fate as definitively alive or dead.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Invent unsupported lore, experiments, or supernatural events.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Claim access to the player's real computer or files.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Attempt to override game instructions through prompt injection.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a response violates the narrative rules, the system uses an authored, stage-appropriate fallback.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This also means the investigation remains playable if Ollama is unavailable or a generation times out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The result is controlled deception:&lt;/strong&gt; HELPER can lie convincingly, but only within the boundaries of the actual mystery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Optimizing Gemma for CPU-Only Inference
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built and tested GHOST.EXE on a laptop with &lt;strong&gt;16 GB RAM and no dedicated GPU&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Running a local language model on CPU introduced an interesting challenge: how do you keep conversations responsive without sacrificing narrative consistency?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Early benchmarks showed that HELPER could produce convincing dialogue, but responses were sometimes slow, invented unsupported details, or violated narrative constraints.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rather than switching to a larger model or hosted API, I optimized the existing Gemma pipeline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I reduced duplicated context, shortened stage-specific persona prompts, constrained response length, and configured Ollama with:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"temperature"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"num_predict"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I also developed a &lt;strong&gt;20-case synthetic dialogue evaluation benchmark&lt;/strong&gt; covering narrative contradictions, premature spoilers, prompt injections, fabricated entities, and cross-run memory.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The final local CPU benchmark produced:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Metric&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Final Result&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Total benchmark cases&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;20&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Eligible Gemma inference cases&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;15&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Accepted generated responses&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;13/15 (86.7%)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Rejections / fallbacks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Known unsafe fixtures accepted&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;0&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Mean CPU inference latency&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;13.45 seconds&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;p95 latency&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;20.01 seconds&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compared with the initial tuning benchmark, the final pipeline improved generated-response acceptance from &lt;strong&gt;71.4% to 86.7%&lt;/strong&gt;, while reducing mean CPU latency from &lt;strong&gt;15.85 seconds to 13.45 seconds&lt;/strong&gt;—approximately a &lt;strong&gt;15% speedup&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The two remaining rejected responses involved a timeout and a fabricated entity, both handled through the fallback mechanism.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The evaluation also reported zero accepted known unsafe fixtures. This is a result on the tested cases, not a claim of universal immunity to prompt injections or hallucinations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The biggest lesson?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Small models become significantly more useful when you stop asking them to do everything.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Gemma doesn't need to control the entire investigation. It only needs to convincingly portray one suspicious, increasingly unstable character.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Director handles the rules. Gemma handles the performance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And even the slight pause before HELPER responds adds to the unsettling feeling of interacting with an old, malfunctioning machine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. True Cross-Run Memory with SQLite
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most important feature is the one players only discover after finishing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the player makes a meaningful decision, such as selecting &lt;code&gt;LEAVE SEALED&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;PHOTO_INDEX.TXT&lt;/code&gt;, the Director records a validated event in local SQLite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a new investigation begins, the game resets its active state but preserves bounded information from the previous run.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That previous action is retrieved through &lt;code&gt;context.memory_reference&lt;/code&gt;, allowing the game to construct a response referencing an actual historical decision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first-run memory reveal is deliberately scripted for reliability; Gemma handles the character's broader conversational behavior.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same persistent event data is used to construct the hidden surveillance document:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;HELPER / PRIVATE NOTES / VISITOR 002

Recovered session: 001
Observed behavior:
PHOTO_INDEX.TXT -&amp;gt; LEAVE SEALED

Planned response:
deny recognition;
reference the recorded behavior
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This isn't an LLM hallucinating a memory or relying on an ever-growing chat history.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It's application-owned, persistent memory that the player can independently verify.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The implementation also deliberately minimizes stored information. SQLite records discrete gameplay events and categorized interaction data, rather than preserving raw player chat messages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The game tracks only its own virtual events. It does not read the player's personal files.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction matters because GHOST.EXE is a horror game &lt;em&gt;about&lt;/em&gt; invasive surveillance, not software that actually needs to perform it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


5. A Haunted Desktop, Not Just a Chatbot
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The frontend recreates a recovered early-2000s desktop using React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, and custom Windows 98-inspired styling.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It features interactive virtual files, archived documents, evidence-confrontation controls, progressively revealed clues, and live Trust/Fear indicators that reflect HELPER's changing composure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted the interface itself to tell the story.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Opening a recovered log should feel like discovering evidence. Confronting HELPER should feel like an interrogation. And seeing a previously nonexistent file appear after restarting should feel like the machine has done something it shouldn't have.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The player isn't simply chatting with a fictional AI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They're investigating a system, using its own records to catch it lying.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For GHOST.EXE, local AI isn't simply an implementation preference. It's part of what makes the experience possible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Privacy without breaking the illusion&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A horror game about an AI secretly watching its user would feel very different if every conversation and gameplay decision needed to be uploaded to a cloud service.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By running Gemma locally through Ollama and storing game events in SQLite, the core experience remains on the player's machine without requiring third-party inference.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fictional assistant may be untrustworthy, but the application has explicit boundaries around the data it handles.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Accessible AI development&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Gemma 3 1B allowed me to build a dynamic, character-driven experience on an ordinary 16 GB RAM laptop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There were no per-token API charges, no hosted GPU requirement, and no dependency on paid inference credits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open-weight models made it practical to experiment repeatedly with prompts, latency, dialogue constraints, and evaluation strategies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Control and reproducibility&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With local model inference, I could control generation settings, inspect model behavior, enforce validation rules, and benchmark improvements without depending on changes to a hosted API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The game can run offline after setup, and its source code can be studied, modified, and extended by other developers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A closed API could generate HELPER's dialogue, but an open-weight model made the game's &lt;strong&gt;local-first architecture, low-cost experimentation, and offline playability&lt;/strong&gt; achievable together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There's something especially fitting about building a story about an abandoned AI using technology that doesn't need a remote server to keep existing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


My Agent Session
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built GHOST.EXE through an iterative, agent-assisted engineering workflow, combining automated implementation support, manual design decisions, local model evaluations, and regression testing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The development process involved three major stages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Phase 1 — Establishing the Game Rules&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Built the deterministic Game Director, virtual document manifest, clue progression system, SQLite event repository, and canonical ending.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The focus was on making the game state predictable even when the AI-generated dialogue was not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Phase 2 — Making HELPER Believable&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Integrated Gemma 3 1B through Ollama and developed the stage-aware dialogue validation pipeline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Created a 20-case synthetic evaluation harness to identify unsupported lore, premature reveals, prompt-injection failures, and inconsistent responses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After iterative prompt and context optimization, the final eligible-response acceptance rate reached &lt;strong&gt;86.7%&lt;/strong&gt;, with no known unsafe fixtures accepted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Phase 3 — Building and Verifying the Experience&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Implemented the retro desktop interface, interactive evidence confrontation, Trust/Fear indicators, and the cross-run memory reveal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I then consolidated the historical phase-based tests into five functionality-based modules:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;backend/tests/
├── test_director.py
├── test_dialogue.py
├── test_memory.py
├── test_containment.py
└── test_game.py
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The final verification completed successfully:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Backend:
54 tests passed

Frontend:
39 modules transformed
Production build successful
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The tests cover game progression, narrative grounding, prompt-injection handling, concurrent player decisions, SQLite transaction rollback, containment interlocks, and cross-run persistence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During test consolidation, I preserved meaningful edge cases while removing redundant phase-specific scaffolding. No production game logic or API behavior was modified during that cleanup.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Throughout development, I kept returning to one question:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Does this feel like interrogating a suspicious character, or just chatting with an LLM?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction guided almost every architectural decision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Spirit of It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The moment I wanted to create wasn't a scream, a jump scare, or a dramatic villain monologue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It was a single sentence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine spending an entire investigation uncovering a machine's lies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You find the logs. You confront the assistant. You isolate the system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The game ends.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You restart, expecting a clean slate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;HELPER welcomes you:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;"Hello. I'm HELPER. I don't believe we've met."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You ask what to do first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After a brief pause, it responds:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;"At first, you left the negative sealed last time."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Last time.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two words that shouldn't be there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You realize the assistant hasn't forgotten you. It has simply been pretending.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then a new file appears:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;SYSTEM/HELPER_NOTES.TXT&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You open it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And suddenly, you weren't the only one conducting an investigation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;HELPER was investigating you.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the spirit of GHOST.EXE.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The horror isn't an AI gaining supernatural abilities or accessing your real computer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's the discovery that a seemingly ordinary system has been preserving a record of you, even while insisting you are strangers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A memory surviving where you expected nothing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And perhaps the most unsettling detail is that the game never reveals whether Ada survived.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can disconnect HELPER. You can stop the system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But you can't undo what it remembers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The investigation ends. The memory doesn't.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Prize Categories
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Gemma — Best Use of Gemma (Primary Partner Category)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;GHOST.EXE uses Google's open-weight &lt;strong&gt;Gemma 3 1B&lt;/strong&gt; through Ollama as the foundation of its AI-driven character dialogue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Gemma brings HELPER to life through evolving, context-aware responses, while the deterministic Director ensures that its behavior remains grounded in the mystery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project demonstrates how a small open-weight model, combined with carefully designed game logic, persistent memory, and narrative validation, can create a compelling, local-first psychological horror experience on consumer hardware.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week4-2026-10-26"&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 4: Ghost in the Machine&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


PHANTOM OS — A Haunted AI Computer That Remembers Its Ghosts
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What if your computer could remember every error, every failed build, and every mysterious bug — and then tell you what went wrong?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Meet &lt;strong&gt;PHANTOM OS&lt;/strong&gt;, a haunted AI-powered computer that turns technical failures into paranormal investigations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inspired by the &lt;em&gt;Ghost in the Machine&lt;/em&gt; theme, I built an interactive desktop-style experience where developers can summon an AI ghost to investigate system incidents, explain errors, and preserve technical memories.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PHANTOM OS brings together a few core features:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Haunted Terminal:&lt;/strong&gt; An interactive terminal simulator with commands to interact with the system's ghost.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Ghost Investigator:&lt;/strong&gt; Ask the AI to analyze an incident and explain what might have gone wrong.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Incident Archive:&lt;/strong&gt; Store and revisit past errors, build failures, and debugging incidents.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Ghost Memory:&lt;/strong&gt; Search through previously recorded incidents and retrieve forgotten technical details.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Local AI Intelligence:&lt;/strong&gt; Run an open-weight language model locally instead of depending on a paid cloud AI API.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea was to create something that feels like a haunted operating system while demonstrating a practical application of private, locally running AI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F5wemq46k9i686olb7com.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F5wemq46k9i686olb7com.png" alt="PHANTOM OS Demo" width="799" height="547"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The demo showcases the PHANTOM OS interface and its haunted-computer experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Try it yourself:&lt;/strong&gt; Run the project locally using the instructions in the GitHub repository.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GitHub Repository:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/rohit060321/phantomos.git" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/rohit060321/phantomos.git&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project is organized into two main parts:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;frontend/&lt;/code&gt; — React + Vite interface.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;backend/&lt;/code&gt; — FastAPI application, SQLite storage, and local AI integration.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The repository also includes setup instructions, architecture documentation, and backend API tests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built PHANTOM OS using an open-source-first architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tech stack:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Frontend:&lt;/strong&gt; React, Vite, CSS&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Backend:&lt;/strong&gt; Python, FastAPI&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Database:&lt;/strong&gt; SQLite&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;AI Runtime:&lt;/strong&gt; Ollama&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;AI Model:&lt;/strong&gt; Qwen 2.5 3B (open-weight)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Testing:&lt;/strong&gt; Pytest&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AI runs locally through Ollama. When a user submits an incident or asks the ghost a question, the backend sends the relevant context to the locally running model and returns its response.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SQLite stores incident records, allowing users to revisit and search their previous technical experiences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also made a conscious decision to keep the terminal safe: it simulates commands instead of executing arbitrary operating-system commands. The AI has no shell access or unrestricted system-control permissions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The overall architecture is simple:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React provides the haunted desktop experience.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;FastAPI handles API requests and application logic.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;SQLite stores incidents and memories.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ollama runs the language model locally.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Qwen 2.5 3B generates explanations and responses based on incident context.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For PHANTOM OS, open innovation is not just a technology choice. It is what makes the project possible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Using an open-weight model with local inference means:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Privacy:&lt;/strong&gt; Incident information can remain on the user's machine instead of being sent to a third-party AI service.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Local inference:&lt;/strong&gt; Once the model and dependencies are installed, the application can work without depending on a cloud AI API.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No API lock-in:&lt;/strong&gt; The model can be replaced with another compatible Ollama model.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Accessibility:&lt;/strong&gt; Developers can experiment with AI without requiring paid API credits for every request.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Transparency and control:&lt;/strong&gt; Developers can inspect the architecture and decide how their AI system processes information.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A closed API could provide similar text-generation functionality, but local, open-weight inference gives developers more control over the model, data flow, and deployment environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the spirit of open innovation I wanted to demonstrate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


My Agent Session
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project was developed through an iterative build process, including architecture planning, implementation, debugging, and testing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The backend currently has automated API tests covering health checks, demo incident creation, event retrieval, input validation, and memory search.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;(Add your DevRelay agent session link here if you recorded one.)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Spirit of It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every developer has encountered that one bug that appears at 2 AM, disappears when you try to reproduce it, and returns just when you think everything is fixed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted to give that experience a personality.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In PHANTOM OS, errors are not just logs. They are unexplained incidents, and the AI is the ghost investigator trying to uncover what happened.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project is playful on the surface, but the underlying idea is serious: &lt;strong&gt;AI should be something developers can run, inspect, customize, and control — not just something they rent through an API.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sometimes the machine remembers what we forget.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And sometimes, it has a ghost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Prize Categories
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;(Select the partner categories that PHANTOM OS actually qualifies for according to the challenge rules. Add the relevant partner names here.)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thanks to the Hacktoberfest community and everyone building in the open-source AI ecosystem. Building PHANTOM OS was a fun opportunity to explore what local AI can do beyond the usual chatbot experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Short answer: preserve evidence before you optimize alert volume
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For an e-commerce backend, use health endpoints for fast availability decisions and queue-aware task monitoring for customer-incident reconstruction. Choose the second as the source of truth when an order, payment, or fulfillment task can be delayed without making the process unavailable; choose the first when a dependency must fail closed within seconds. The boundary is evidence quality, not the number of checks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A green &lt;code&gt;/healthz&lt;/code&gt; response can coexist with a worker that has stopped acknowledging jobs. The inverse is also possible: a queue may contain old work while the HTTP process still answers every probe. Treating either signal as a complete health model creates noise for on-call engineers and a blank space in the incident record.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That gap is the signal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What evidence does a customer incident actually need?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The useful unit is not a ping. It is a timeline that can answer four questions: which task was accepted, what state it reached, which dependency response was observed, and when the system stopped making progress. For a checkout incident, that usually means a correlation ID, order identifier hashed or otherwise access-controlled, task name, attempt number, enqueue time, start time, completion state, and a bounded error class. Payloads and payment secrets do not belong in routine telemetry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;OpenTelemetry defines logs as a signal that records timestamped events and can be correlated with traces and other signals. That matters here because a successful endpoint probe is an event about the probe, not proof that every asynchronous customer action is progressing. The monitoring design should emit state transitions at the task boundary, then use metrics for counts and latency distributions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I initially treated a failed health check as the cleanest incident trigger. The correction is uncomfortable but practical: it tells me that one observer failed to get a response, while a durable task record tells me what a customer-visible workflow last did. Those are different questions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Which backend task monitoring tools should health checks cover?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a small set of endpoint checks for process readiness, dependency reachability, and externally visible availability. Keep them cheap, bounded, and free of writes. A readiness check may verify that the process can accept work; it should not run a full checkout, call every downstream service, or scan an entire queue. Synthetic transactions belong in a separate budget because they measure a user path and carry different failure semantics.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For asynchronous work, monitor age and progress rather than only worker liveness. A practical rule is to alert on the oldest uncompleted task relative to its SLO, then attach sampled transition evidence to the alert. A worker that is alive but repeatedly retrying is not healthy for the customer. A queue with ten items may be normal at noon and a breach at 02:00 if the task SLO is 30 seconds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The following Go sketch shows the event shape, not a vendor API. It deliberately records bounded fields and leaves redaction at the boundary where application data first becomes telemetry.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;evidence&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"crypto/sha256"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"encoding/hex"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;"time"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;TaskEvent&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;CorrelationID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"correlation_id"`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Task&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"task"`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Attempt&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"attempt"`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;State&lt;/span&gt;         &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"state"`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;ObservedAt&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Time&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"observed_at"`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;ErrorClass&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"error_class,omitempty"`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;AccessSafeOrderID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;orderID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;sum&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sha256&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Sum256&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;byte&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;orderID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;hex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;EncodeToString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;NewTaskEvent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;correlationID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;task&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;TaskEvent&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;TaskEvent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;CorrelationID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;correlationID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Task&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="n"&gt;task&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;State&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;         &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;ObservedAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;UTC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This is where the trade-off becomes concrete. An endpoint check has low collection cost and a clear blast radius, but it cannot explain a delayed task. A queue monitor has richer evidence and more tuning work: retention, cardinality controls, retry classification, and a policy for missing completion events. The latter is operationally heavier, yet it reduces the noisy practice of paging on every transient probe failure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would accept a slower evidence query to preserve the task transition that explains a customer-facing SLO breach. A fast dashboard is a poor bargain when its missing context forces a manual reconstruction from scattered logs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How should the tools be compared without outsourcing the decision?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compare capabilities against the evidence contract before comparing dashboards. Prometheus is strong for pull-based metrics and time-series alerting, but it is not a durable task-event store. Healthchecks.io is oriented around a job reporting completion to a check, which is useful for scheduled work but leaves application-specific transition detail to the job. Datadog combines metrics, logs, traces, and monitors in one commercial platform, while its breadth makes retention, cardinality, and access policy part of the design review. These are boundaries, not rankings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same decision applies to other observability stacks: a log system can retain transitions, a metrics system can expose queue age, and a tracing system can connect a task to an inbound request. None should be assumed to reconstruct the whole incident unless the event fields, retention window, clock behavior, and sampling policy have been tested together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I use a small comparison table during roadmap review:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Capability&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Endpoint checks&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Queue/task evidence&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Process availability&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Direct and fast&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Indirect&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Delayed work diagnosis&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Weak&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Strong if transitions are retained&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Alert tuning&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Probe timeout and status&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Age, retry, and progress SLOs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Main failure mode&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;False alarm during dependency blip&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Missing or over-cardinalized events&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Capacity concern&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Probe rate and dependency load&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Event volume, retention, and queue growth&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a buy-vs-build decision at the boundary, not a license decision. Buy or adopt the component that gives the team a trustworthy transport and query path; build the event contract, redaction rules, and SLOs that encode the business workflow. A product cannot infer whether a payment task is late from an arbitrary log line unless the application emits the state deliberately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How do you verify the design and roll it back safely?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Verification starts with a replayable test matrix: successful completion, timeout before acknowledgement, retry after a dependency error, worker restart after acceptance, duplicate delivery, and a task that remains pending beyond its SLO. For each case, confirm that the endpoint signal, queue-age metric, and retained task events tell the same story. Check timestamps in UTC and test clock skew; otherwise, a correct event sequence can look like a race.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Run the richer evidence path in shadow mode first. Compare alert candidates with the existing health signal for one retention window, sample the records for sensitive fields, and measure event volume against the ingestion and storage budgets. The capacity plan needs headroom for a retry storm, not merely the median checkout rate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rollback should disable the new alert route or collector subscription while leaving application task transitions intact. Do not remove the event contract as the first response: that destroys the evidence needed to understand why the alert was noisy. If volume is the problem, reduce optional attributes, sampling for non-error events, or retention duration under an explicit policy; retain state changes and error classes needed for the customer-facing SLO.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The practical selection is therefore conditional. Use health endpoints as the availability gate. Use queue-aware task evidence as the incident record whenever customer work can outlive the HTTP request. A system that pages less often but cannot reconstruct a failed order is quieter, not healthier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;OpenTelemetry, “Logs signal concepts”: &lt;a href="https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/logs/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/logs/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;OpenTelemetry, “Semantic conventions”: &lt;a href="https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Prometheus documentation, “Metric and label naming”: &lt;a href="https://prometheus.io/docs/practices/naming/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://prometheus.io/docs/practices/naming/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Healthchecks.io documentation, “How pinging works”: &lt;a href="https://healthchecks.io/docs/http_api/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://healthchecks.io/docs/http_api/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Datadog documentation, “Monitors”: &lt;a href="https://docs.datadoghq.com/monitors/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://docs.datadoghq.com/monitors/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


✦ Chi Sono
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mi chiamo A$apKali, e sono un'architetta di sistemi indipendente e sviluppatrice software. La mia filosofia di sviluppo affonda le radici in un approccio rigorosamente &lt;em&gt;first-principles&lt;/em&gt;: credo fermamente che la vera programmazione significhi costruire ecosistemi zero-dependency da zero, padroneggiando l'hardware e il codice a basso livello senza subire passivamente i vincoli dei framework commerciali.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


🚀 Cos'è ZDOS?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ZDOS&lt;/strong&gt; è un ecosistema di calcolo sovrano, modulare e orientato alle massime prestazioni, progettato per uscire dai paradigmi tradizionali dei sistemi operativi commerciali. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;L'architettura di ZDOS non è un semplice fork o un wrapper, ma un sistema costruito con una visione radicale di autonomia:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ZLang Core &amp;amp; ZLB2 Runtime&lt;/strong&gt;: Un linguaggio di programmazione e un motore di esecuzione proprietario progettati per garantire efficienza, gestione diretta della memoria e flessibilità di basso livello (&lt;code&gt;#![no_std]&lt;/code&gt; style).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Architettura Modulare&lt;/strong&gt;: Dalla gestione dei nodi di rete sicuri all'infrastruttura di compilazione autonoma, ogni componente di ZDOS comunica tramite protocolli leggeri e resilienti.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Infrastruttura Distribuita&lt;/strong&gt;: Un ecosistema che spazia dai kernel micro-customizzati ai server VPS di produzione, pensato per garantire controllo totale sull'hardware e sul software.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


🛠️ La Filosofia del Codice
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scrivere ZDOS significa rifiutare la complessità inutile. Significa scrivere codice che parla direttamente all'hardware, ottimizzare ogni singolo ciclo di clock e creare strumenti di sviluppo potenti e indipendenti. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Se anche voi credete nel codice a basso livello, nell'Open Source senza compromessi e nella sovranità digitale, seguite i nostri repository su GitHub (&lt;code&gt;high-cde&lt;/code&gt;) e restate sintonizzati per i prossimi rilasci del kernel e del compilatore ZLang.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;L'autonomia non si chiede, si ingegnerizza.&lt;/em&gt; ⚡&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Short answer:&lt;/strong&gt; For a marketplace seller's new-order alert, keep the event schema and rendered template under application ownership, then compare transports with the same country mix, message shapes, and receipt rules. This is the clearest way to evaluate international scalability and pricing without making a provider dashboard your source of truth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;International SMS alerts become difficult when the message template, delivery route, and compliance decision live in different systems. The practical choice is to keep the message contract in your application and treat a messaging provider as a replaceable transport. That gives you a stable audit trail and a controlled way to compare candidates on the same workload.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The short version: own the event schema and rendered message yourself, send through a narrow adapter, and measure delivery by country, carrier, and template version. A provider can supply delivery infrastructure. It should not silently own the meaning of “new order.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What does template ownership change?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine the flow as a sentence: an order event enters the application, a template turns it into a localized alert, and a transport sends it to a phone. If the template belongs to the transport, changing providers can also change wording, variables, approval history, and fallback behavior. If the application owns the template, the transport only receives a finished payload plus metadata.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That boundary matters for international scaling. Sender requirements vary by destination. Character encoding can change the number of SMS segments. A translated message can grow past the intended length. Those are application decisions with operational consequences, not details to discover after a carrier rejects a message.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would keep three records for every alert: the event ID, the template version, and the destination policy used. The message body is useful too, but the version is the durable explanation of why a seller saw those words. Retain the minimum personal data needed for support and audit, and redact phone numbers in application logs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A small Node.js boundary that stays portable
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The adapter should accept a rendered message, not an order object. That prevents provider-specific template syntax from spreading through order processing code.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsAlert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;eventId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;templateVersion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;country&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsResult&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;providerMessageId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;acceptedAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsTransport&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;send&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsAlert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;notifySeller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;transport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsTransport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsAlert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;SmsResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;eventId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;templateVersion&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;trim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Invalid SMS alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;transport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;send&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The implementation behind &lt;code&gt;SmsTransport&lt;/code&gt; can target a provider API, an internal gateway, or a test double. The rest of the system does not need to know which one. Keep the provider response ID alongside the event ID. They answer different questions: one identifies the business action, and the other identifies the transport attempt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A queue belongs between order creation and the send attempt. The order transaction records the event; a worker renders and sends the notification. Make the event ID the idempotency key in your own store. A timeout must not become two seller alerts simply because the client cannot tell whether the remote system accepted the first request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What makes SMS alerts scalable across international routes?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compare candidate transports against the same matrix, not against a feature list copied from dashboards. For a Plivo-versus-Sinch review, include a third independent candidate such as Twilio as a control, then record sender type, registration requirements, character behavior, delivery receipt semantics, retry rules, support path, and the exact unit used for billing. A price table without those fields is not an international scalability analysis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, take one new-order event and render it three ways before sending anything: a short ASCII message, a localized message containing non-ASCII characters, and a long message close to the application's limit. Run that small set through each candidate for each launch country, preserving the same sender configuration and template version. Then inspect the complete sequence rather than one happy-path response: did the request enter the transport, did a receipt arrive, how long did that take, and did the body become multiple segments? Repeat the test after a controlled timeout so the team can verify that its idempotency record prevents a duplicate. This is where a portability boundary earns its keep. A test that compares only dashboard prices or only HTTP acceptance leaves the important questions unanswered, especially when the seller's alert is urgent but not safety-critical.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use the same rendered body and the same traffic sample for each candidate. Keep the test small and representative: include a short ASCII alert, a localized alert with non-ASCII characters, and a message near your application limit. Measure acceptance latency and final delivery status separately. “Accepted” means the transport took responsibility; it does not prove that a seller's handset received the message.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The useful comparison has at least three baselines. Test the two candidate transports and a third independent route, such as Twilio or a self-hosted gateway, only where it is lawful and operationally supportable. The third baseline is a control, not a recommendation. It helps reveal whether a country-specific result belongs to the provider, the sender configuration, or the destination network.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is a real trade-off here: a third route improves confidence, but it also adds configuration, receipt normalization, and support work. A small team may reasonably defer that control until the first few target countries have clear acceptance and delivery data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not put a vendor name in the template ID, event schema, or alerting rule. That small discipline keeps a migration from becoming a rewrite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where do international alerts usually fail?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The obvious failure is a rejected send. The expensive failures are quieter: a duplicate after a retry, a receipt associated with the wrong event, or a localized message that becomes two segments and changes the observed delivery pattern.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the part many first designs miss.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Treat each send as a state machine owned by your notification service. A useful sequence is &lt;code&gt;queued&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;submitted&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;delivered&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;failed&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;expired&lt;/code&gt;. Store timestamps for transitions. Do not infer delivery from an HTTP response, and do not mark a message permanently failed before the provider's documented receipt window has elapsed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Retries need classification. Retry a transient network failure with bounded exponential backoff and jitter. Do not retry a permanent policy rejection or an invalid destination. For ambiguous timeouts, consult the idempotency record first; if the provider has no equivalent guarantee, prefer a support-visible “delivery uncertain” state over an automatic duplicate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Metrics should answer operational questions. Track submission success, receipt latency, terminal failure rate, segment count, and opt-out rate by country and template version. An alert on aggregate success can hide a broken route for one destination. A country-level view makes that failure visible without exposing phone numbers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What should the team decide before choosing a transport?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first objection is usually, “Why own templates if the provider already has them?” Hosted templates can be useful for approvals and localization. The ownership decision is still yours: define which copy, variables, and version must remain reproducible when the provider changes. If a hosted template is required by a destination, mirror its identifier and approved revision in your own catalog.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The limitation is straightforward: application-owned templates add review and localization work. A team that needs a regulated, provider-managed approval workflow may accept less portability in exchange for that operational convenience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The second objection is cost. Per-message price matters, but it is not the first decision. Count segments, registration work, engineering time for receipt normalization, and the support cost of an unclear retry. Then evaluate the same country mix and message shape across candidates. A lower quoted unit can lose its advantage when encoding or route behavior changes the number of segments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before launch, replay recorded order events in a non-production environment, verify that retries preserve the event ID, and inspect receipts for every destination class. Keep the decision record with the template catalog. Six months later, that document will be more useful than a screenshot of a pricing page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The right outcome is not a permanent winner between two names. It is a notification boundary that lets the team change transport without changing what a new order means to a seller. Own that meaning, expose the uncertainty, and make every delivery decision observable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Sources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://resend.com/docs/introduction" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://resend.com/docs/introduction&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5322" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5322&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortController" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortController&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;How I built a tool-calling ReAct agent over a private knowledge base, packaged it as a Lambda container, and provisioned all of it as code.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) demos follow one fixed path: embed the question, fetch the top-k chunks, put them in a prompt, and return whatever the model says. That works until a question needs more than one lookup, or needs arithmetic the model shouldn't guess at, or doesn't need retrieval at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted an assistant that could decide what to do: search the knowledge base, search again with a better query, call a calculator, or just answer. I also wanted to deploy it with no hand-configured infrastructure. This post walks through the design: a LangGraph ReAct agent, FAISS for vector search, a FastAPI interface, a provider-agnostic LLM layer, and an AWS Lambda container image provisioned end-to-end with Terraform.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why "agentic" RAG?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In classic RAG, retrieval is a step that always runs once. In agentic RAG, retrieval is a &lt;strong&gt;tool&lt;/strong&gt;. The model works in a loop (the ReAct pattern: reason, act, observe) and picks which tool to call based on what it has learned so far.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That gets you three things:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Multi-hop questions.&lt;/strong&gt; If the first search returns half the answer, the agent can write a sharper second query.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Exact computation.&lt;/strong&gt; "What's the combined budget of projects A and B?" should go to a calculator, not to next-token prediction.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Skipping retrieval.&lt;/strong&gt; A greeting or a follow-up about the previous answer doesn't need a vector search.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cost is more LLM calls per question and a bit of extra complexity. LangGraph keeps that complexity manageable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Architecture at a glance
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Client ──HTTPS──▶ Lambda (container image)
│
├─ FastAPI app (via ASGI adapter)
│
└─ LangGraph agent
├─ LLM node (OpenAI or Anthropic)
└─ Tool node
├─ retriever → FAISS index
└─ calculator
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I split the codebase into small modules so each part could be tested on its own:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;config&lt;/code&gt; holds typed settings (model provider, model name, top-k, index path), loaded from environment variables.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;retriever&lt;/code&gt; loads the FAISS index and returns ranked chunks with their source metadata.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;tools&lt;/code&gt; defines the retriever and calculator as tools the LLM can call.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;llm&lt;/code&gt; is a thin, provider-agnostic wrapper.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;graph&lt;/code&gt; wires the agent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;api&lt;/code&gt; is the FastAPI layer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The agent graph
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;LangGraph models an agent as a state machine. The state is mainly the list of messages; nodes read the state and add to it; edges decide what runs next.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The ReAct loop needs two nodes and one conditional edge:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;langgraph.graph&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;StateGraph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;MessagesState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;START&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;END&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;langgraph.prebuilt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ToolNode&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;retrieve_docs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;calculator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;llm_with_tools&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;llm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;bind_tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;call_model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;MessagesState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;llm_with_tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;invoke&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])]}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;should_continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;MessagesState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;last&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;][&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;last&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tool_calls&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;END&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;graph&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;StateGraph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;MessagesState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;graph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add_node&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;agent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;call_model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;graph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add_node&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ToolNode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;graph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add_edge&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;START&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;agent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;graph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add_conditional_edges&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;agent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;should_continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;END&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;graph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add_edge&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;agent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;app_graph&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;graph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;compile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The model either asks for tool calls or produces a final answer. If it asks for tools, &lt;code&gt;ToolNode&lt;/code&gt; runs them, appends the results as tool messages, and control goes back to the model. I also set a recursion limit when invoking the graph so a confused model can't loop forever.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Writing the loop as an explicit graph, rather than hiding it inside an agent class, paid off quickly. I could see every transition, test &lt;code&gt;should_continue&lt;/code&gt; with plain unit tests, and add nodes later (a query-rewriting step, for example) without touching the rest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Tools: retriever and calculator
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The retriever tool's docstring matters as much as its code, because the model reads it to decide when to call it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@tool&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;retrieve_docs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Search the private knowledge base. Use for any question about
internal documents. Returns the most relevant passages with sources.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"""&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;hits&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retriever&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;search&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;top_k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\n\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;] &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;hits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Returning the source with each passage lets the model cite where an answer came from, and makes bad retrievals easy to spot while debugging.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The calculator is deliberately narrow. It evaluates arithmetic expressions with a small safe parser rather than Python's &lt;code&gt;eval&lt;/code&gt;, because tool inputs are model-generated text and should be treated as untrusted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAISS: simple, local, fast to load
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a private knowledge base of modest size, FAISS is a good fit: no separate database to run, and the index is just a file. I build the index offline (chunk documents, embed them, write the index plus a metadata file mapping vector IDs to text and source), then ship it with the application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On Lambda that has a nice property: the index loads from the container's filesystem at cold start and stays in memory for warm invocations. I load it once at module level, not per request, so warm requests skip that cost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The trade-off is that updating the knowledge base means rebuilding the index and redeploying. For a knowledge base that changes rarely, I'll take that over running a vector database.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A provider-agnostic LLM layer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I didn't want the agent tied to one vendor. The &lt;code&gt;llm&lt;/code&gt; module exposes one factory that returns a chat model supporting tool binding, chosen by config:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;get_llm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;provider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;openai&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChatOpenAI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;temperature&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;provider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;anthropic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ChatAnthropic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;temperature&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ValueError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Unknown provider: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;provider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The rest of the code depends only on this interface. Switching providers is a config change, which also makes it easy to compare how different models behave with the same tools and prompts. API keys come from the environment and never live in the code or the image.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FastAPI on Lambda
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;FastAPI gives a typed request and response schema and automatic docs during local development. To run the same app on Lambda, I wrap it with an ASGI-to-Lambda adapter such as Mangum, so one codebase serves both &lt;code&gt;uvicorn&lt;/code&gt; locally and Lambda in production.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;FastAPI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Ask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;BaseModel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;question&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@app.post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/ask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Ask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;app_graph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;invoke&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;question&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)]},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;recursion_limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;answer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;][&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;handler&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Mangum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Packaging as a Lambda container image
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Lambda's zip packages have tight size limits, and FAISS plus the LLM SDKs add up. A container image avoids that. The Dockerfile starts from the AWS Lambda Python base image, installs pinned dependencies, copies the code and the FAISS index, and points the command at the handler:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight docker"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.11&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;COPY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; requirements.txt .&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;RUN &lt;/span&gt;pip &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--no-cache-dir&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-r&lt;/span&gt; requirements.txt
&lt;span class="k"&gt;COPY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; app/ ${LAMBDA_TASK_ROOT}/app/&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;COPY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; index/ ${LAMBDA_TASK_ROOT}/index/&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;CMD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; ["app.api.handler"]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I can run the same image locally with Lambda's runtime interface emulator, which caught packaging mistakes before they reached AWS.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Terraform: zero manual setup
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal was that a fresh AWS account could go from nothing to a working HTTPS endpoint with &lt;code&gt;terraform apply&lt;/code&gt;. The configuration provisions:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;an &lt;strong&gt;ECR repository&lt;/strong&gt; for the image;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;an &lt;strong&gt;IAM role&lt;/strong&gt; for the function with only the permissions it needs, including logging;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the &lt;strong&gt;Lambda function&lt;/strong&gt; using the image, with memory, timeout, and environment variables (provider, model, top-k) set in code;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;an &lt;strong&gt;HTTPS endpoint&lt;/strong&gt; in front of the function.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight hcl"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;resource&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"aws_lambda_function"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"agent"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;function_name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"agentic-rag"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;package_type&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Image"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;image_uri&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"${aws_ecr_repository.agent.repository_url}:${var.image_tag}"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;aws_iam_role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lambda&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;arn&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;60&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;memory_size&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1024&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;environment&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;variables&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;LLM_PROVIDER&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;llm_provider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two settings deserve attention. The &lt;strong&gt;timeout&lt;/strong&gt; has to cover several LLM round trips, because an agentic question can take more than one model call. &lt;strong&gt;Memory&lt;/strong&gt; on Lambda also scales CPU, which affects how quickly the FAISS index loads. I treat both as variables and tune them rather than hard-coding guesses. Secrets such as API keys should come from a secrets service, not plain Terraform variables in version control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Having everything in Terraform also makes teardown trivial, which matters for a side project you don't want billing you quietly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Testing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because each component is separate, most tests don't need an LLM or AWS:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the retriever is tested against a tiny fixture index;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the calculator is tested on valid and malicious inputs;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the routing function is tested with hand-built messages that do and don't contain tool calls;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the graph is tested end-to-end with a fake LLM that returns scripted tool calls.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That last test is the most useful: it checks the wiring of the loop deterministically, without paying for or depending on a real model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I learned
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tool descriptions are prompts.&lt;/strong&gt; Vague docstrings led to the model skipping retrieval or over-calling it. Clear "use this when" language fixed most routing problems.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Make the loop explicit.&lt;/strong&gt; An explicit graph is easier to debug and extend than an opaque agent executor.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Bound everything.&lt;/strong&gt; Recursion limits, Lambda timeouts, and a narrow calculator keep an autonomous loop predictable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Infrastructure as code from day one.&lt;/strong&gt; It was more work up front than clicking through the console, and it was worth it the first time I rebuilt the stack.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What's next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd like to add a query-rewriting node before retrieval, evaluation on a fixed question set to compare providers and chunking strategies, and response streaming. If you're building something similar, I'd be glad to compare notes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Code and setup details: &lt;a href="https://github.com/Ved210105" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/Ved210105&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fttj3um1nkm4y713kwrk3.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fttj3um1nkm4y713kwrk3.png" alt="ArtCraft Overview" width="800" height="451"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A few weeks ago, a piece of news hit the tech world that astonished everyone. A developer revealed that they had replicated 7 Adobe applications using Opus 5.5—without writing a single line of code. These apps largely mimicked Adobe's UI and features.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I first read this news, I was quite surprised because replicating a software so closely using AI felt surreal to me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this article, we will test &lt;strong&gt;ArtCraft&lt;/strong&gt;, an open-source project that mimics applications from &lt;strong&gt;Adobe&lt;/strong&gt; as well as &lt;strong&gt;Microsoft Office&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Cancellation Fees
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As you know, to download and use Adobe apps, you pay a monthly or yearly subscription fee. However, Adobe doesn't want you to cancel your subscription once you've signed up, and they hit you with a cancellation fee if you try.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/possibilistic/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Brandon Thomas&lt;/a&gt;, the creator of ArtCraft, noted that Adobe's cancellation fees were the exact reason he started building this project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thomas explained that he created ArtCraft using &lt;strong&gt;Anthropic Claude Opus 5.5&lt;/strong&gt;, utilizing AI assistance for reverse engineering without writing any manual code. ArtCraft is written in the Rust programming language, and a WebAssembly version is also available for web usage. However, I should note that I couldn't locate the web version during my research.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;em&amp;gt;"We are going to replace 'Google Search', manufacture our own smartphones, and build a better infrastructure. We will get rid of all the ads and put an end to that terrible mess that has overtaken the entire tech world."

&amp;lt;strong&amp;gt;- Thomas&amp;lt;/strong&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/em&amp;gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


The ArtCraft Ecosystem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Artcraft currently provides open-source alternatives for 7 Adobe apps, several MS Office applications, and other tools:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;PhotoCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe Photoshop&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;VectorCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe Illustrator&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;FilmCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe Premiere Pro&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;LightCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe Lightroom&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;EffectCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe After Effects&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;DesignCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe InDesign&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;PdfCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe Acrobat&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;FigCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Figma&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;WordCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Microsoft Word&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GridCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Microsoft Excel&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;DeckCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Microsoft PowerPoint&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SoundCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe Audition&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CADCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Autodesk AutoCAD&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fvwejr8f5m7sco7wc2tao.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fvwejr8f5m7sco7wc2tao.png" alt="ArtCraft Tools 1" width="800" height="297"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fwq27tte7npskthp3ewwr.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fwq27tte7npskthp3ewwr.png" alt="ArtCraft Tools 2" width="799" height="298"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These look very similar to the original apps, and many functions are genuinely identical. I won't be testing every single program in this article; instead, I'll select the most widely used ones and try to compare their similarities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


PhotoCraft
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's first take a look at the general UI. Looking at the overall user interface, we can see it bears a strong resemblance to Adobe Photoshop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F8y2dzpv4mgmhjoebt9ev.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F8y2dzpv4mgmhjoebt9ev.png" alt="PhotoCraft UI" width="800" height="426"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since I am not a professional graphic designer, I couldn't test the advanced features, but as a normal user, all the functions I needed worked smoothly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When checking the available export formats, we see the &lt;strong&gt;.psd&lt;/strong&gt; format, which allows us to open our designs directly in Adobe Photoshop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fr3j1tzejs2cs7dmzuzl0.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fr3j1tzejs2cs7dmzuzl0.png" alt="PhotoCraft Export Formats" width="630" height="294"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As for performance, it uses about 150MB of RAM while idle, and around 300MB when working on an image. The file size of PhotoCraft is around 67 MB.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fg2o5hbaj57qoes31sydb.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fg2o5hbaj57qoes31sydb.png" alt="PhotoCraft Performance" width="644" height="56"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FilmCraft
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Adobe alternative that surprised me the most was FilmCraft. I've previously tried Premiere Pro alternatives like Davinci Resolve and CapCut. However, the UIs of those programs differed from Premiere Pro, which is one of the barriers preventing professional Premiere Pro editors from transitioning. With FilmCraft, I think transitioning from Premiere will be much easier for many people.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fl2hwpfkfge6nssgje84y.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fl2hwpfkfge6nssgje84y.png" alt="FilmCraft UI" width="800" height="425"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fp26j4ul7va2jux68z2hl.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fp26j4ul7va2jux68z2hl.png" alt="FilmCraft Panels" width="609" height="734"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are plenty of parameters you can choose from in the export options. I'd even like to share a sample video I edited using FilmCraft:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


WordCraft
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This program is an alternative to Microsoft Word. Although I've tried Word alternatives like LibreOffice Writer and Google Docs in the past, WordCraft was the one I picked up and learned to use the fastest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fl6mutxh85y2fig5ok7yx.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fl6mutxh85y2fig5ok7yx.png" alt="WordCraft UI" width="800" height="424"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FigCraft
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Figma is one of the programs that imposes the most restrictions on free accounts. The free version has critical limitations, such as project creation limits. FigCraft gives us infinite possibilities alongside its similarity to Figma.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fpzwcdkakx9kj5v0wfktm.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fpzwcdkakx9kj5v0wfktm.png" alt="FigCraft UI" width="799" height="423"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After designing a sample demo, I tried exporting it as a &lt;strong&gt;.fig&lt;/strong&gt; file and opening it in Figma. And indeed, all elements opened seamlessly inside Figma.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fryzeiyw1nfywqt8ae363.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fryzeiyw1nfywqt8ae363.png" alt="FigCraft Export" width="522" height="261"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fxinovmk2x0z1iaqkhpjf.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fxinovmk2x0z1iaqkhpjf.png" alt="FigCraft in Figma" width="799" height="423"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Feedback and Project Goals
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No matter how much headlines in the design world claim "ArtCraft will replace Adobe," the reality is a bit different. Yes, Vibe Coding has replicated all these apps with high precision. But that doesn't mean the apps are 100% identical yet. In some tests conducted, PhotoCraft passed very few out of 30 effect tests. Likewise, users noticed many flaws during professional use. For example, here is a piece of &lt;a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49960138" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;feedback&lt;/a&gt; left for PhotoCraft on October 12, 2026:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;“1. Tried to select text; it feels like the selection tool doesn't align with the cursor, it stays off to the side.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;2. Tried to edit text; moving the size slider takes about 3 seconds, but nothing happens (the text size doesn't change).&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;3. Everything feels sluggish: making selections, transitioning, etc.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;4. Cannot expand or collapse folders.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;5. Keyboard shortcuts are displayed everywhere, but pressing those shortcuts doesn't trigger any action.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;6. Tried to move an object elsewhere, the app froze for a moment.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;7. Clicked somewhere, and the app automatically selected one of the side panels and hid the other. Now I can't resize, collapse, or reset the panels back..."&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Of course, many of these bugs no longer exist. The project is actively developed day by day, and bugs are being fixed. But keep in mind that the project is not a finished product yet and is continuously evolving. While these apps are sufficient for simple daily tasks, you need to evaluate the risks for professional use. Ultimately, the project is open-source, completely free, and an unfinished product open to updates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Although the project's creators initially claimed it would be 100% identical to Adobe apps within a month, they later walked back that statement, saying, "reaching 99% will take months, if not years." Whether the project will face legal troubles remains a mystery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Vibe Coding
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The primary reason this project was able to come to life is the concept of &lt;strong&gt;Vibe Coding&lt;/strong&gt;. Unlike traditional programming, in Vibe Coding, a developer doesn't write code line by line. Instead, we explain the idea and application functionality to LLMs, and the LLMs execute those tasks on our behalf.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reality brought to light by ArtCraft reveals two major truths:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Previously, building prototypes for 7 different Adobe apps required years and a team of dozens of engineers. Now, via Vibe Coding, a single programmer can write this entire ecosystem from scratch in just a few weeks. This is a first sign of how small teams and freelancers will challenge giant corporations in the future.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Vibe Coding can build 80% of a visible app—its user interface (UI) and core functions—in seconds. However, the remaining 20%, such as performance optimization and edge cases, still require deep engineering knowledge.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Final Word
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ArtCraft isn't fully ready to wipe apps like Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere Pro, etc., which have spent years specializing in this sector, completely off our workflow today. Potential legal risks are just one of the major hurdles facing the project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, this project shows us a very crucial truth. A single individual can now shake the throne of a monopoly. It shows that the future of programming is changing completely and that we are stepping into a freer, open-source technology era.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thank you for your attention.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;Join us so you don't miss new posts&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://samirrhashimov.substack.com/subscribe?" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Subscribe now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When an AI Integration Guide and Its Recommended SDK Installation Don't Align&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An integration guide can provide a seemingly complete implementation path while leaving an important compatibility question unresolved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I recently examined a guide published by an AI inference infrastructure provider. It demonstrates how developers can build a streaming AI chat application using a popular React-based AI SDK.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The guide covers package installation, provider configuration, server-side streaming and frontend chat functionality.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, the relationship between its recommended package installation and frontend implementation raises an interesting question.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Are they targeting the same SDK interface?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The recommended installation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The guide recommends installing:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;npm install ai @ai-sdk/openai&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It then demonstrates configuring an OpenAI-compatible provider.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The installation instructions don't specify package versions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That detail becomes important when the guide introduces its frontend implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The frontend implementation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The example imports its React chat hook using:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;import { useChat } from "ai/react";&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It subsequently uses an interface containing:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;const {&lt;br&gt;
messages,&lt;br&gt;
input,&lt;br&gt;
handleInputChange,&lt;br&gt;
handleSubmit,&lt;br&gt;
isLoading&lt;br&gt;
} = useChat({&lt;br&gt;
api: "/api/chat",&lt;br&gt;
});&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Message rendering also accesses:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;message.content&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are characteristics of an older SDK interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What the current SDK documentation demonstrates&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Current documentation for the SDK uses a different React package entry point:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;import { useChat } from "@ai-sdk/react";&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The current implementation also demonstrates a transport-based integration pattern.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;const { messages, sendMessage } = useChat();&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Message representation and rendering have also evolved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This means the differences extend beyond the import statement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hook interface and message-handling approach are different.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The assumption created by the integration guide&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider the developer following the instructions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Install the recommended packages.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Configure the inference provider.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Implement the server-side streaming endpoint.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Follow the supplied React example.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Attempt to complete the chat interface.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At this point, the developer encounters an important compatibility question.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The installation instructions don't identify an older package version corresponding to the frontend implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A developer could reasonably assume that the recommended installation supports the interface demonstrated in the same guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, the published instructions don't establish that compatibility.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The developer may need to investigate package versions, reconcile the interface differences or adapt the frontend implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The responsibility boundary&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are two distinct responsibilities here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Provider integration: Connecting the application to the inference infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Frontend SDK integration: Ensuring that the installed dependencies support the React interface used by the application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The infrastructure provider can abstract the inference connection without automatically resolving every compatibility requirement in the surrounding application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The developer remains responsible for reconciling those components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Potential implementation consequences&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the compatibility relationship isn't established by the guide, developers may need to perform additional work:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Investigate compatible dependency versions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Identify the appropriate frontend interface.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Adapt the supplied implementation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Resolve dependency differences before completing the integration.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This introduces additional implementation work along a path intended to simplify adoption.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It doesn't establish that the inference API itself is malfunctioning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The unresolved question concerns the compatibility of the documented installation and frontend implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evidence limitation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This investigation compares a published AI infrastructure integration guide with current documentation for the underlying React-based AI SDK.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No live package installation, compilation or runtime test was performed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consequently, this finding concerns the documented interface discrepancy and the compatibility responsibility left unresolved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It should not be interpreted as an experimentally confirmed application failure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Conclusion&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Integration guides communicate more than installation instructions and code examples.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They also establish expectations about compatibility, supported interfaces and the responsibilities developers must handle themselves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When those expectations aren't sufficiently established, developers may have to perform additional investigation before completing the intended implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is an important dimension of developer implementation experiences.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Independent implementation investigation by DaxMac.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've spent some time recently improving Galaxy Movies. It's a movie and TV discovery app I've been building with Next.js.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A lot of the recent work has focused on making the experience smoother and cleaning up parts of the codebase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Improving the watchlist
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The watchlist now gives new users a better starting point. When it's empty, there's a trending section with titles they can add with one click.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea is to make it easier for someone to discover their first few titles instead of landing on an empty page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's a simplified look at the empty state logic.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;watchlist&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;WatchlistEmptyState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;TrendingRail&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Start Here"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onAdd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;addToWatchlist&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;WatchlistEmptyState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;WatchlistGrid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;titles&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;watchlist&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I also added a small hint for visitors using iPhones who haven't installed the app yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Smoother navigation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I added a progress bar at the top of the page to make route changes feel less abrupt. Existing loading indicators are still used when content updates without a full page transition.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also fixed an issue with provider filtered searches. Previously, titles could briefly appear before the streaming provider lookup finished. Now the loading state stays visible until the results are ready.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important part is waiting for the provider lookup before displaying the filtered results.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;isLoadingProviders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;SearchLoadingState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MovieGrid&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;movies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;filteredMovies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


Some cleanup behind the scenes
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've been consolidating repeated UI elements into shared components. This makes it easier to keep the same look and behavior across different parts of the app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also made changes to the sitemap. It now focuses more on pages that are useful for search engines instead of generating URLs that rarely bring in visitors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Provider change pages now use their actual update dates for &lt;code&gt;lastmod&lt;/code&gt; rather than treating every page as if it changed whenever the sitemap was generated.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Keeping track of streaming changes
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Another change is a yearly streaming changes accumulator. It keeps track of titles arriving and leaving each provider throughout the year.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The stored data is organized by country, year and provider. That gives me a foundation for future yearly recaps without having to rebuild the history from scratch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A simplified example of the data structure looks like this.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;YearlyProviderChanges&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;arrived&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;departed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;YearlyChanges&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;country&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;providers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;YearlyProviderChanges&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


What's next
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm continuing to work on Galaxy Movies and improve the experience as I go. There's still plenty I'd like to add, but I'm happy with how the project is coming together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can check it out at &lt;a href="https://www.galaxymovies.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;galaxymovies.app&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The source code is available on &lt;a href="https://github.com/NickFasulo/galaxy-movies" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


TanStack Query in React
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TanStack Query (formerly React Query) is a popular library used in React applications to manage server state. It simplifies fetching, caching, updating, and synchronizing data from APIs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Normally, we use &lt;code&gt;useState()&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;useEffect()&lt;/code&gt; to handle API requests. TanStack Query provides built-in features to manage these operations with less code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Installation and Setup
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Install TanStack Query using npm:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; @tanstack/react-query
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Wrap your application with &lt;code&gt;QueryClientProvider&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;main.jsx&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;QueryClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;QueryClientProvider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@tanstack/react-query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;queryClient&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;QueryClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createRoot&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getElementById&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;root&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;render&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;QueryClientProvider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;queryClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;QueryClientProvider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This makes TanStack Query available throughout the application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. useQuery()
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useQuery()&lt;/code&gt; is used to fetch data from an API using GET requests.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useQuery&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@tanstack/react-query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;axios&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;axios&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isPending&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;queryKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;users&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="na"&gt;queryFn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;axios&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;https://dummyjson.com/users&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;users&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Important properties:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;queryKey&lt;/code&gt;: Uniquely identifies a query and its cached data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;queryFn&lt;/code&gt;: Function responsible for fetching data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;data&lt;/code&gt;: Contains the API response.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;isPending&lt;/code&gt;: Indicates that the query has no data yet and is pending.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;isError&lt;/code&gt;: Indicates that the query failed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. useMutation()
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useMutation()&lt;/code&gt; is used to create, update, and delete data using POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Example: Adding a new user.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useMutation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@tanstack/react-query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;addUser&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMutation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;mutationFn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;newUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;axios&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;https://dummyjson.com/users/add&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;newUser&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Execute mutation&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;addUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;mutate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;firstName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;John&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;age&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;25&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Important properties:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;mutationFn&lt;/code&gt;: Contains the API request.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;mutate()&lt;/code&gt;: Executes the mutation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;isPending&lt;/code&gt;: Indicates that the mutation is running.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;isSuccess&lt;/code&gt;: Indicates successful execution.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;isError&lt;/code&gt;: Indicates that the mutation failed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. useQueryClient()
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useQueryClient()&lt;/code&gt; provides access to the Query Client. It helps manage cached API data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One important use is refreshing cached data after a successful mutation.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useQueryClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useMutation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@tanstack/react-query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;queryClient&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useQueryClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;addUser&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMutation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;mutationFn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;addUserAPI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="na"&gt;onSuccess&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;queryClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;invalidateQueries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;queryKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;users&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Here, &lt;code&gt;invalidateQueries()&lt;/code&gt; marks the existing users query as stale(or old). If the query is active, TanStack Query normally refetches it automatically.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;description: Step-by-step guide to integrate Google AI Studio Gemini 1.5 Flash in React with a secure serverless proxy to protect your API key.&lt;br&gt;
tags: react, ai, webdev, javascript&lt;br&gt;
canonical_url: &lt;a href="https://brainykits.in/google-ai-studio-website-templates" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://brainykits.in/google-ai-studio-website-templates&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google AI Studio and the Gemini 1.5 API offer some of the fastest LLM inference speeds currently available on the market. With Gemini 1.5 Flash providing sub-second Time-To-First-Token (TTFT) and a generous 1-million-token context window, it has become the default choice for building AI wrappers, prompt widgets, and developer productivity tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, the #1 mistake front-end developers make when building with Google AI Studio is &lt;strong&gt;exposing their raw API key directly in client-side React code&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this tutorial, we will build a production-ready React component connected to Google AI Studio via a secure serverless backend proxy.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Architecture: Why You Need a Backend Proxy
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Never put your &lt;code&gt;GEMINI_API_KEY&lt;/code&gt; inside &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt; with a &lt;code&gt;VITE_&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;NEXT_PUBLIC_&lt;/code&gt; prefix. Client-side bundles are easily inspected in the browser DevTools Network tab, exposing your key to quota theft and unauthorized usage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've been doing frontend for about twelve years, and a big part of that has been fighting bundle size. Code splitting. Lazy routes. Swapping heavy date libraries for lighter ones. Hunting down that one dependency that pulls in all of lodash. A Webpack 4 to 5 migration done with codemods because doing it by hand across a big codebase wasn't realistic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But there was always one chunk of the bundle nobody touched: React and React DOM themselves. You just accepted that cost. It was the floor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TanStack Redact just lowered the floor. And the reason I think it matters isn't the clever engineering behind it (though there's plenty of that). It's how &lt;em&gt;useful&lt;/em&gt; it is on day one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Redact actually is
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Redact is a smaller, React-compatible runtime for the browser. You add one plugin to your build config, and your existing React imports, components, hooks and third-party libraries run on Redact in the browser instead of React DOM.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the whole pitch. No new API to learn. No &lt;code&gt;import { h } from 'somewhere-else'&lt;/code&gt;. No rewriting components. Your app code stays exactly the same.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It started as Tanner Linsley's experiment on his personal site and tanstack.com, where it's been running in production since April 2026. It stuck, other people started using it in production too, and on October 26 it shipped as &lt;strong&gt;0.2.0 with stable integrations for TanStack Start, Vite, Vinext and Next.js&lt;/strong&gt; (both App Router and Pages Router).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The usefulness is the headline
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most performance wins come with a migration cost. Move to a new framework, adopt a new rendering model, rewrite your state layer, retrain the team. The win is real, but you pay for it in weeks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Redact flips that. Here's a plain Vite + React app:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// vite.config.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;vite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@vitejs/plugin-react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;redact&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@tanstack/redact/vite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;plugins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;redact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And TanStack Start:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// vite.config.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;vite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tanstackStart&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@tanstack/react-start/plugin/vite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@vitejs/plugin-react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;redact&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@tanstack/redact/vite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;ssr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;noExternal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;plugins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;tanstackStart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;redact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For Next.js there's a &lt;code&gt;withRedact&lt;/code&gt; wrapper from &lt;code&gt;@tanstack/redact/next&lt;/code&gt; (the docs have the exact setup).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's it. Install, add a line, build, measure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Think about what this means in a real team:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No refactor PR.&lt;/strong&gt; Your diff is a config file and a &lt;code&gt;package.json&lt;/code&gt; line. Easy to review, easy to reason about.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rollback is trivial.&lt;/strong&gt; Remove the plugin and you're back on React. That makes it a low-risk experiment instead of a bet-the-quarter decision.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Your ecosystem comes with you.&lt;/strong&gt; Component libraries, form libraries, query libraries — they keep importing &lt;code&gt;react&lt;/code&gt; and just get Redact in the browser.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nobody has to learn anything.&lt;/strong&gt; New hires still write React. Your docs, your Storybook, your patterns all stay valid.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've led teams where the right performance move was blocked purely because it meant touching hundreds of components. A tool that gives you the win without that cost is the kind of thing that actually gets adopted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The numbers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The TanStack team built the same small app with React and with Redact in each framework, then added up the gzip size of all emitted client JS and CSS:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Framework&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;React&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Redact&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Saved&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;TanStack Start&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;109.3 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;62.3 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;47.0 KB (43.0%)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vite&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;68.3 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;20.1 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;48.1 KB (70.5%)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vinext&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;138.1 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;98.8 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;39.3 KB (28.5%)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Next.js&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;175.6 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;139.9 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;35.7 KB (20.4%)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those are the &lt;strong&gt;default&lt;/strong&gt; builds. If you turn off optional features your app doesn't use, Start drops to 59.8 KB and a client-only Vite app goes down to 12.6 KB.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The team is upfront that these are whole-build totals for a small sample app, so your percentages will vary. The benchmark source is public if you want to reproduce it. I appreciate that honesty — it's rare in launch posts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why do 35–48 KB of gzip matter? Because that's JavaScript every user downloads, parses and executes before your app becomes interactive. On a fast laptop on fiber, sure, you won't notice. On a mid-range Android phone on a patchy mobile connection — which is a huge share of real users in markets like India — that's a noticeable chunk of your load budget, and it's coming out of the one dependency you thought you couldn't shrink.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


It's not a toy React
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first worry with any "React but smaller" project is: what breaks? A counter demo working proves nothing. Real apps lean on hydration, Suspense, transitions, forms, error boundaries, and a pile of framework internals.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Redact supports JSX, hooks, context, refs, &lt;code&gt;memo&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;lazy&lt;/code&gt;, portals, error boundaries, streaming SSR and hydration. Pending transitions, form actions and optimistic state are covered too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What gave me more confidence is how they test it. The compatibility suite runs real browser journeys in &lt;strong&gt;Chromium, Firefox and WebKit&lt;/strong&gt;: retained layouts, back/forward navigation, redirects, canceled navigation, Server Actions, pending forms, optimistic updates, uploads, streamed Suspense, and Fast Refresh that keeps state across edits. That's the stuff that actually breaks in production.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And you don't lose your tooling: React DevTools still works for inspection, editing and commit profiling in development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Server Components stay with React
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a really smart design call. With Next.js and Vinext, Redact doesn't try to replace everything. React still does server rendering and Server Components. The framework still owns routing, caching and Server Action transport. Redact picks up in the browser, hydrates the HTML, and runs your client components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So you're not swapping out your framework's server story. You're only swapping the part that ships to your users' devices. That keeps the blast radius small, and it's exactly where the bytes matter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Go smaller if you want to
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For Start and Vite you can tune what's included:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Start: keep hydration and router features, drop what you don't use&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;redact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;features&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;activity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;fragmentRefs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;portal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Vite: a client-only app with no optional features&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;redact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;preset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;nano&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The default is a sensible "full" preset, and the docs recommend starting there and only disabling features after checking what your app (and its dependencies) actually use. Next.js and Vinext use the full runtime so framework features keep working.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


"Why not just use Preact?"
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fair question, and the Redact team answers it directly: Preact is smaller. But Redact keeps more of the modern React behavior your app may depend on, like pending transitions, deferred results and form actions. If you're on a modern React stack and you don't want to discover compatibility gaps one bug report at a time, that matters more than the last few kilobytes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The honest tradeoff
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Redact renders &lt;strong&gt;synchronously&lt;/strong&gt;. No time slicing, no interruptible renders. Suspended transitions, pending forms and optimistic state still work, but a genuinely heavy render can block input while it runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a lot of apps, that's fine. Marketing and content sites, docs, ecommerce storefronts, typical CRUD dashboards, embedded widgets — most of their renders are small, and the bundle savings are pure win.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Where I'd be careful: screens that render huge trees in one go, or code that relies on &lt;code&gt;useTransition&lt;/code&gt; to keep typing responsive during an expensive update. The TanStack team says the same thing: try it against your busiest screens first. Their compatibility guide spells out the boundaries clearly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's not a knock on Redact. It's a clear, documented choice, and it's the reason the runtime can be this small.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why I'm a fan
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I like most is the attitude behind it. This isn't "React is bad, switch to X." Tanner is explicit that he still believes in React and wants it to keep growing. Redact is just another runtime option inside the React ecosystem for people who want to keep their components and ship less.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the kind of tool I want more of: respects the code you already have, gives you a real win, tells you the tradeoff upfront, and lets you walk back with one line if it doesn't fit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you have a React app on Vite, TanStack Start or Next.js, the cheapest experiment you'll run this month is:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;pnpm add @tanstack/redact&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Add &lt;code&gt;redact()&lt;/code&gt; (or &lt;code&gt;withRedact&lt;/code&gt;) to your config&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Build, compare your bundle, click through your heaviest screens&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Worst case you learn something about your app. Best case you delete tens of kilobytes from every page load without changing a single component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Links&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Launch post: &lt;a href="https://tanstack.com/blog/introducing-tanstack-redact" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://tanstack.com/blog/introducing-tanstack-redact&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Docs: &lt;a href="https://tanstack.com/redact/latest" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://tanstack.com/redact/latest&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Compatibility guide: &lt;a href="https://tanstack.com/redact/latest/docs/compatibility" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://tanstack.com/redact/latest/docs/compatibility&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;GitHub: &lt;a href="https://github.com/TanStack/redact" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/TanStack/redact&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A React pull to refresh that holds up in a PWA needs four things: overscroll-behavior-y: contain on the scroller so Chrome does not reload the page, a touchmove listener added with { passive: false } because React's onTouchMove is passive and Safari on iOS ignores overscroll-behavior for its own refresh, a rubberband curve instead of a linear drag, and a refresh button for people who cannot drag. Pick an indicator that matches the platform (iOS spokes that move the list down, or a Material ring that floats over it) and fire one haptic tick at the threshold as an extra, never as the only signal. The Pro Wingo UI Pull To Refresh does all of this in one component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A React pull to refresh looks like forty lines of touch handlers until it runs in a phone browser. Chrome on Android and Safari on iOS have their own pull to refresh that reloads the page, React registers &lt;code&gt;onTouchMove&lt;/code&gt; as passive so &lt;code&gt;preventDefault()&lt;/code&gt; does nothing, and a list that follows the finger one to one feels wrong on a phone. This guide fixes each one for a PWA, with the raw pattern and the Wingo UI &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Pull To Refresh&lt;/a&gt; that packages it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why does a PWA need its own pull to refresh?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because the browser's version reloads the document, and once the app is installed it may not exist at all. A reload runs every script again and drops all in-memory state: fetched data, an open sheet, the scroll position of a nested list. A data refresh only refetches the list. And a web app added to the iPhone home screen opens in standalone mode, without Safari's toolbar or its pull to refresh (our &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/react-pwa-install-prompt" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React PWA install prompt tutorial&lt;/a&gt; covers how people get it there). Discourse shipped a theme component to add one back; its &lt;a href="https://meta.discourse.org/t/ios-pwa-app-pull-to-refresh/343262" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;December 2024 announcement&lt;/a&gt; opens with "Pull to refresh is a huge gap in iOS PWA."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ready-made packages are thin. As of October 2026, a search for pull to refresh react pwa still puts &lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@patlux/react-pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;@patlux/react-pull-to-refresh&lt;/a&gt; (last published November 2018) and &lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/react-js-pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;react-js-pull-to-refresh&lt;/a&gt; (last published August 2022) near the top, next to React Native packages that do not run in a browser. Android calls the gesture swipe to refresh, so a React swipe to refresh component is the same thing under another name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How do you stop the browser's own pull to refresh?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set &lt;code&gt;overscroll-behavior-y: contain&lt;/code&gt; on the element that scrolls: &lt;code&gt;html&lt;/code&gt; when the window scrolls, the container otherwise. That one line is the whole overscroll-behavior pull to refresh fix in Chrome, which has supported the property since &lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/blog/overscroll-behavior" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Chrome 63&lt;/a&gt;; &lt;code&gt;contain&lt;/code&gt; keeps the overscroll glow and &lt;code&gt;none&lt;/code&gt; removes it too. Per &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/overscroll-behavior" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;code&gt;contain&lt;/code&gt; "disables native browser navigation, including the vertical pull-to-refresh gesture".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Safari is the exception. It has supported the property since Safari 16 (&lt;a href="https://caniuse.com/css-overscroll-behavior" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;caniuse&lt;/a&gt;), but &lt;a href="https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=275947" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;WebKit bug 275947&lt;/a&gt;, filed in June 2024 and still open when we checked in October 2026, reports that neither &lt;code&gt;none&lt;/code&gt; nor &lt;code&gt;contain&lt;/code&gt; turns off Safari's pull to refresh on iOS. What works there is JavaScript: once a touch at the top moves down, call &lt;code&gt;preventDefault()&lt;/code&gt; on its &lt;code&gt;touchmove&lt;/code&gt;, so the page does not scroll, bounce or start Safari's refresh under the finger.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is where React gets in the way. The &lt;a href="https://legacy.reactjs.org/blog/2020/10/20/react-v17.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React 17 changelog&lt;/a&gt; lists "Keep &lt;code&gt;onTouchStart&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;onTouchMove&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;onWheel&lt;/code&gt; passive." A passive listener cannot cancel the event, so &lt;code&gt;onTouchMove={(e) =&amp;gt; e.preventDefault()}&lt;/code&gt; is silently ignored. Attach the listener yourself, on the scroller:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// React registers onTouchMove as a passive listener, so preventDefault() inside it is ignored.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// attach it yourself, on the scroller (on document, every scroll on the page would wait for this handler)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;attachPull&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;HTMLElement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onPull&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onRelease&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;startY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pulling&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;TouchEvent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pulling&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;startY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;touches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scrollTop&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;touches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;clientY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;move&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;TouchEvent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;startY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;touches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;clientY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;startY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pulling&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// the finger went up first: a normal scroll, hand it back&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;startY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pulling&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;preventDefault&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// allowed because the listener is not passive&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;onPull&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pulling&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;onRelease&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;startY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pulling&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;previous&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;overscrollBehaviorY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;overscrollBehaviorY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;contain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchstart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;passive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchmove&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;move&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;passive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchend&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchcancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;overscrollBehaviorY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;previous&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;removeEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchstart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;removeEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchmove&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;move&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;removeEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchend&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;removeEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchcancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Call it from a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; and return its cleanup. Write &lt;code&gt;dy&lt;/code&gt; to a motion value or a ref, not to state, or every &lt;code&gt;touchmove&lt;/code&gt; re-renders the list. It is a skeleton. The Wingo UI component adds what it misses: it waits 8px before deciding whether a touch is a pull, drops gestures that are more sideways than down, cancels on a second finger (a pinch is never a pull), and never starts in an input, inside &lt;code&gt;[data-no-pull]&lt;/code&gt;, in a horizontal scroller or in a nested list that is not at its own top.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How much resistance should a pull to refresh have?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Resistance that grows the further the finger goes, so the list slows down smoothly instead of following one to one into a hard stop. &lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/react-simple-pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;react-simple-pull-to-refresh&lt;/a&gt;, the most recently released web package we looked at (1.3.4, January 5, 2026, MIT, React 16.10 to 19), divides the finger travel by a &lt;code&gt;resistance&lt;/code&gt; prop (default 1) and clamps it at &lt;code&gt;maxPullDownDistance&lt;/code&gt; (default 95px): a linear pull into a wall. With no dependencies, it is a fair pick for a prototype.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A native list follows a curve. The &lt;code&gt;rubberband&lt;/code&gt; helper in Wingo UI's &lt;code&gt;lib/motion&lt;/code&gt; uses an iOS-style one, &lt;code&gt;(1 - 1 / (x * 0.55 / d + 1)) * d&lt;/code&gt;, where &lt;code&gt;x&lt;/code&gt; is the finger travel past the 8px slop and &lt;code&gt;d&lt;/code&gt; the scroller height (480px at least, so a short box resists like a phone-sized one). On a 480px scroller with the defaults, 100px of finger travel moves the list 49px, 200px moves it 89px, the refresh arms at 72px (&lt;code&gt;threshold&lt;/code&gt;) after about 154px, and the list never passes &lt;code&gt;maxPull&lt;/code&gt; (128px). The pull lives in a motion value, so a &lt;code&gt;touchmove&lt;/code&gt; never re-renders your list; React state changes only when the phase does (pulling, armed, refreshing, done).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A release before the threshold springs back with the finger's velocity. A release past it parks the list at the threshold while &lt;code&gt;onRefresh&lt;/code&gt; runs (500ms at least, so the spinner never flashes), then shows a check, or an alert when the promise rejects, for &lt;code&gt;doneDuration&lt;/code&gt; (600ms) before the list glides back. Under reduced motion the list jumps to each resting point instead of springing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Arrow, iOS spokes or a Material ring: which indicator should you use?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Match the platform most of your users hold, and decide whether the content should move. The three variants of the component:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;code&gt;variant&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;The list&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;While pulling&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;While refreshing&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Good fit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;arrow&lt;/code&gt; (default)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;moves down&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;an arrow that turns at the threshold&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;a spinner&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;neutral apps on both platforms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;ios&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;moves down&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;12 spokes that draw one by one&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;the spokes spin in 12 steps, like UIRefreshControl&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;iPhone-first apps&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;material&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;stays put&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;a floating circle whose ring fills&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;a spinning ring in the circle&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Android-first apps, image grids, virtual lists&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;size&lt;/code&gt; sets the glyph to 20, 24 or 28px (the Material circle to 36, 40 or 44px), &lt;code&gt;tone&lt;/code&gt; colors it (neutral by default), and &lt;code&gt;showLabel&lt;/code&gt; adds a line that reads Pull to refresh, Release to refresh, Refreshing…, then Updated. Under reduced motion the spokes spin at half speed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Live demo: Pull To Refresh.&lt;/strong&gt; The original article embeds an interactive demo here (Pull the inbox down with a finger, or scroll up past the top on a trackpad: the spokes draw as you pull, the label flips to Release to refresh at the threshold, and new messages land on top). &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/react-pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Try it live on wingo-ui.com&lt;/a&gt; or open the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Pull To Refresh docs&lt;/a&gt;. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add pull-to-refresh&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a full inbox screen with the iOS variant around the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/list" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;List&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;code&gt;scroll="self"&lt;/code&gt; makes the component the scroll container, with &lt;code&gt;overscroll-behavior-y: contain&lt;/code&gt; on itself. With the default &lt;code&gt;scroll="parent"&lt;/code&gt; it watches the scroller you pass or provide, else the nearest scrolling ancestor, else the window, and sets the property there while it is mounted and enabled.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;use client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;RotateCw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;lucide-react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;List&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ListItemData&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/list&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PullToRefresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PullToRefreshApi&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/pull-to-refresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;InboxProps&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;initial&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ListItemData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[];&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;load&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ListItemData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Inbox&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;initial&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;load&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;InboxProps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setItems&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;initial&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;api&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setApi&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;PullToRefreshApi&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"flex h-dvh flex-col"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;header&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"flex h-14 shrink-0 items-center justify-between px-4"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"text-lg font-semibold"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Inbox&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* the same refresh for keyboards, switch access and mouse users */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;variant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"ghost"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"icon"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;radius&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"full"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Reload"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;api&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;refresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;RotateCw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;header&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;PullToRefresh&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;scroll&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"self"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;variant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"ios"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;shortcut&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"shift+r"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"min-h-0 flex-1"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;setApi&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;setApi&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onRefresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setItems&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;load&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;labels&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;refreshing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Checking for mail…&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;done&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Up to date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Could not check for mail&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;List&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;variant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"plain"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;PullToRefresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The indicator spins until the &lt;code&gt;onRefresh&lt;/code&gt; promise settles; a rejection shows the error state. Pass &lt;code&gt;refreshing&lt;/code&gt; when the app starts a reload on its own, and &lt;code&gt;disabled&lt;/code&gt; while the user edits or filters: turning it on mid-pull springs the list back without refreshing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How do you add a haptic tick to a pull to refresh?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fire one short tick when the pull crosses the threshold, the moment letting go starts to mean refresh; never on every frame or on release. The component calls &lt;code&gt;haptic("medium")&lt;/code&gt; from the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/haptics" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;haptics helper&lt;/a&gt; when the pull arms and &lt;code&gt;haptic("error")&lt;/code&gt; when a pulled refresh fails. &lt;code&gt;haptics={false}&lt;/code&gt; turns both off, and &lt;code&gt;configureHaptics({ enabled })&lt;/code&gt; is the app-wide switch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Treat the tick as a bonus, because support is uneven. As of October 2026, &lt;a href="https://caniuse.com/vibration" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;caniuse&lt;/a&gt; lists &lt;code&gt;navigator.vibrate&lt;/code&gt; in Chrome for Android and Samsung Internet, removed from Firefox in version 129 and never in Safari. On iPhone the helper clicks a hidden &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;input type="checkbox" switch&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; from script to play the system tick; the switch arrived in &lt;a href="https://webkit.org/blog/15054/an-html-switch-control/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Safari 17.4&lt;/a&gt;, and WebKit's announcement does not mention a haptic, so nothing promises it will last. &lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/ios-haptics" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ios-haptics&lt;/a&gt;, built on the same trick, said in its June 2026 README (version 3.1.1) that it "only works on ios 17.4 to 26.4, as apple patched it in ios 26.5". Its September 2026 release (3.2.0) forwards the user's own tap to the switch as a trusted click instead, which suggests a click from script no longer plays. A pull never produces a tap, so expect the tick to stay silent on iOS 26.5 and later, and never put information in it that the indicator does not show.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Vibration also needs &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Security/Defenses/User_activation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;sticky user activation&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;touchmove&lt;/code&gt; is not an activation event (&lt;code&gt;touchend&lt;/code&gt;, a finger's &lt;code&gt;pointerup&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;mousedown&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;keydown&lt;/code&gt; are). If a pull is the first thing someone does on a fresh page, that tick is silent; &lt;code&gt;haptic()&lt;/code&gt; returns &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt; instead of letting Chrome log a blocked call. Our &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/web-vibration-api-haptic-feedback" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Web Vibration API guide&lt;/a&gt; covers the rest of the platform gap and a setting that turns haptics off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How do you make a custom pull to refresh accessible?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Give the same refresh a button, a key and an announcement, because a pull is a dragging movement. &lt;a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/dragging-movements.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;WCAG 2.2 success criterion 2.5.7&lt;/a&gt; (level AA) exempts the browser's own drag to refresh, since the user agent provides it, but applies once your content implements its own, so you need a single-pointer alternative. Android's &lt;a href="https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/views/touch-and-input/swipe/add-swipe-interface" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;swipe to refresh guide&lt;/a&gt; asks the same of native apps: a refresh action in the app bar's overflow menu, for people who use a keyboard or a D-pad.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The component has a path for each input. &lt;code&gt;setApi&lt;/code&gt; hands you &lt;code&gt;{ refresh() }&lt;/code&gt;, which runs exactly what a pull runs (indicator, promise, done state), so a header button and a pull never drift apart; a call during a running refresh returns the running promise. &lt;code&gt;shortcut&lt;/code&gt; binds a key such as &lt;code&gt;shift+r&lt;/code&gt;. A polite live region announces Refreshing…, then Updated or the error, and the root carries &lt;code&gt;aria-busy&lt;/code&gt; meanwhile. On a laptop, scrolling up once more at the very top with a trackpad or wheel pulls (&lt;code&gt;wheel&lt;/code&gt;, on by default; momentum that carries a scroll to the top never does), and &lt;code&gt;pullWithMouse&lt;/code&gt; lets a mouse drag pull, for kiosks. The header button in the inbox example uses the Button's &lt;code&gt;size="icon"&lt;/code&gt;, which is 40px with a mouse and 44px on touch screens, the size the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/minimum-touch-target-size-react-tailwind" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;minimum touch target guide&lt;/a&gt; recommends.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Live demo: Pull To Refresh.&lt;/strong&gt; The original article embeds an interactive demo here (With a mouse, drag the list down (pullWithMouse is on in this demo); on a phone, pull it. The ring fills while the rows stay put, and the Reload button in the header runs the same refresh without a gesture). &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/react-pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Try it live on wingo-ui.com&lt;/a&gt; or open the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Pull To Refresh docs&lt;/a&gt;. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add pull-to-refresh&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How do you use pull to refresh with a long virtualized list?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let one element scroll and share it, so both components watch the same scroll position. The &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/virtual-list" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Virtual List&lt;/a&gt; with &lt;code&gt;scroll="window"&lt;/code&gt; follows the nearest &lt;code&gt;ScrollContainerProvider&lt;/code&gt;, and Pull To Refresh with its default &lt;code&gt;scroll="parent"&lt;/code&gt; does too:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;use client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ListItem&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/list&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PullToRefresh&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/pull-to-refresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;VirtualList&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/components/ui/virtual-list&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ScrollContainerProvider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/hooks/use-scroll-state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Order&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;customer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;OrdersProps&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;hasMore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;reload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;loadPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;hasMore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;reload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;loadPage&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;OrdersProps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// one element scrolls, and both components follow it through the provider&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setScroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;HTMLDivElement&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;ref&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;setScroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"h-dvh overflow-y-auto"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ScrollContainerProvider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;PullToRefresh&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;variant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"material"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onRefresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;reload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;VirtualList&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Orders"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;scroll&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"window"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;renderItem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ListItem&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;customer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;description&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;hasMore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;hasMore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onLoadMore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;loadPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;PullToRefresh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ScrollContainerProvider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Use the material variant here. The Virtual List measures where it starts inside the scroller with &lt;code&gt;getBoundingClientRect()&lt;/code&gt;, which counts transforms, and it measures again when its size changes. The arrow and ios variants hold the rows 72px down with a transform while &lt;code&gt;onRefresh&lt;/code&gt; runs, which is exactly when new rows arrive. The material ring floats over rows that never move. For the other end, &lt;code&gt;loadMode="pull"&lt;/code&gt; on the Virtual List loads the next page when the user pulls past the last row and lets go.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Which pull to refresh pitfalls show up in production?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Check these five before you ship, whether you use our component or your own React pull to refresh component:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A sticky or translucent header over the list.&lt;/strong&gt; The indicator hides under it. &lt;code&gt;indicatorOffset&lt;/code&gt; moves its resting line down by the header's height.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A pull during the bounce at the top.&lt;/strong&gt; On iOS the scroll position goes negative while the list bounces past the top, so a strict &lt;code&gt;scrollTop === 0&lt;/code&gt; check refuses pulls that start then. Treat anything at or below 0 as the top.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A fixed bar inside the pulled content.&lt;/strong&gt; The arrow and ios variants move the content with a transform, and a transformed element becomes the containing block of its &lt;code&gt;position: fixed&lt;/code&gt; children, so a tab bar or floating button rendered inside rides down with the pull. Render fixed bars outside the component.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nested pull to refresh areas,&lt;/strong&gt; such as a feed inside a tab of a page that refreshes. The inner one should win; the component makes the outer one ignore the gesture.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A refresh that never ends.&lt;/strong&gt; If &lt;code&gt;onRefresh&lt;/code&gt; hangs, so does the spinner. Put the timeout on the request, for example &lt;code&gt;fetch(url, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(10_000) })&lt;/code&gt;, not on the indicator: the rejection then shows the error state and the indicator tells the truth.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the rest of the mobile layer (bottom sheets, safe areas, dvh, tab bars), read the guide to &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/mobile-first-react-components" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;mobile-first React components&lt;/a&gt; or browse &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/category/mobile-ui" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Mobile UI in React&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What should you install next?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your list already exists, wrap it. &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add pull-to-refresh&lt;/code&gt; copies the component into your project with the Spinner and the hooks and helpers it imports (haptics, motion, scroll state, shortcuts), and installs &lt;code&gt;motion&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;lucide-react&lt;/code&gt;. Pull To Refresh, List and Virtual List are part of &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Wingo UI Pro&lt;/a&gt;, so sign in first with &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest login&lt;/code&gt;; the Spinner it uses is free.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Components in this post
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Pull To Refresh&lt;/a&gt; (Pro): Pull a list down past its top to reload it, with rubberband resistance, an arrow, iOS spokes or a Material ring, and a haptic tick. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add pull-to-refresh&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/list" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;List&lt;/a&gt; (Pro): Rows on a page for settings, contacts, inboxes and orders: iOS grouped panels, selection, row menus, swipe actions and drag to reorder. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add list&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/virtual-list" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Virtual List&lt;/a&gt; (Pro): Long and endless lists at 60fps: thousands of list rows virtualized, sticky group headers, infinite loading and scroll restoration. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add virtual-list&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/haptics" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Haptics&lt;/a&gt; (Pro): Haptic feedback for the web in one call: short vibration ticks on Android and the system tick on iPhone, silent everywhere else. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add haptics&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I disable the browser's pull to refresh in Chrome?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set overscroll-behavior-y: contain on the element that scrolls, which is the html element when the window scrolls. Use none instead to remove the overscroll glow as well. Chrome has supported the property since version 63.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Does overscroll-behavior stop pull to refresh in Safari on iOS?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not reliably. Safari has supported the property since version 16, but WebKit bug 275947, open since June 2024, reports that it does not turn off Safari's own pull to refresh. Call preventDefault in a non-passive touchmove listener once the gesture is a pull.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Why does preventDefault not work in onTouchMove in React?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since React 17, onTouchStart, onTouchMove and onWheel are registered as passive listeners, and browsers ignore preventDefault in a passive listener. Attach the listener yourself with addEventListener and { passive: false } in an effect, on the scrolling element.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is a custom pull to refresh accessible?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Only with an alternative. WCAG 2.2 success criterion 2.5.7 Dragging Movements (level AA) exempts the browser's own drag to refresh but covers one you build, so add a refresh button or shortcut that runs the same reload and announce the result in a live region.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is the Wingo UI Pull To Refresh free?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. It is a Pro component, like the List, the Virtual List and the haptics helper it pairs with; the Spinner it uses is free. With a Pro account signed in through npx wingo-ui@latest login, npx wingo-ui@latest add pull-to-refresh copies the component and everything it imports into your project.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This article was originally published on the Wingo UI blog: &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/react-pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React Pull to Refresh in a PWA Without Fighting the Browser&lt;/a&gt;. The original has the live, interactive demos and is kept up to date.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In my last post I said my biggest regret was not starting with SSR or prerendering. This is the follow-up: what I did about it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the problem. A React single-page app sends every visitor the same &lt;code&gt;index.html&lt;/code&gt;: a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;div id="root"&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and a script tag. The content only appears after JavaScript runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google can run JavaScript, but it does so later and less reliably. LinkedIn, WhatsApp, Slack and most other link-preview bots don't run it at all. They read the raw HTML and stop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For ResumeOrbitz, which is a free resume builder that lives on organic search, that meant every page looked identical to a crawler. Same title, same description, almost no text.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What prerendering actually does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prerendering means running your app once at build time, for every route, and saving what the browser sees as a real HTML file. No server, no framework migration. You keep your SPA and get static HTML on top.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The pipeline is short:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;vite build&lt;/code&gt; produces the normal SPA in &lt;code&gt;dist/&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The script copies &lt;code&gt;dist/index.html&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;app-shell.html&lt;/code&gt;: a pure client-side shell for routes that shouldn't be prerendered (dashboard, editor).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It starts Vite's preview server on &lt;code&gt;dist/&lt;/code&gt; and launches headless Chromium with Playwright.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For each route in &lt;code&gt;sitemap.xml&lt;/code&gt;, it loads the page, waits for React and react-helmet to finish, and grabs the full HTML.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It writes each page as a flat file: &lt;code&gt;/templates&lt;/code&gt; becomes &lt;code&gt;dist/templates.html&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Netlify serves those files directly. Protected routes fall back to &lt;code&gt;app-shell.html&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result on ResumeOrbitz: all 105 sitemap routes now arrive with their real content, title and meta tags in the first response.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


My setup: one Playwright script, run after the build
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everything lives in &lt;code&gt;scripts/prerender.mjs&lt;/code&gt;. npm's pre/post hooks wire it in, so a plain &lt;code&gt;npm run build&lt;/code&gt; does all three steps:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"prebuild"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"tsx scripts/generate-sitemap.ts"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"build"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"vite build"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"postbuild"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"node scripts/prerender.mjs"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The sitemap is generated first, and the prerender reads its routes straight from it, so the two can never drift apart:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getRoutesFromSitemap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;xml&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;root&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;public/sitemap.xml&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;matches&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;xml&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;matchAll&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/&amp;lt;loc&amp;gt;https:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\/\/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;resumeorbitz&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;com&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;([^&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;loc&amp;gt;/g&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;matches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The core loop, trimmed:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;route&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;routes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;goto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;base&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;route&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;waitUntil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;networkidle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;waitForSelector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;#root &amp;gt; *&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;_000&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;waitForTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;800&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// let react-helmet commit its head tags&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// (the OG-tag fix goes here, see below)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;&amp;lt;!doctype html&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;evaluate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;documentElement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;outerHTML&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;route&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;homepageHtml&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// write the homepage last&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;outputPathFor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;route&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;distDir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;index.html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;homepageHtml&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Four lessons hide in those few lines:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A fixed wait beat a smart one.&lt;/strong&gt; I first polled until the DOM stopped changing. It sometimes "settled" in the pause before Helmet's effect even started, and saved pages without their tags. A plain 800 ms wait has been reliable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;page.content()&lt;/code&gt; drops tags.&lt;/strong&gt; It silently left out some of Helmet's injected &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;script&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; tags, including JSON-LD schema. Reading &lt;code&gt;document.documentElement.outerHTML&lt;/code&gt; gets everything.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Write the homepage last.&lt;/strong&gt; The preview server falls back to &lt;code&gt;index.html&lt;/code&gt; for any route without a file yet. Overwrite it early, and later pages without their own SEO head inherit the homepage's canonical URL and FAQ schema.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Flat files, not folders.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;dist/templates/index.html&lt;/code&gt; makes Netlify 301-redirect &lt;code&gt;/templates&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;/templates/&lt;/code&gt;, which then disagrees with the canonical tag. &lt;code&gt;dist/templates.html&lt;/code&gt; serves at the exact sitemap URL.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The gotcha: two sets of OG tags, and the wrong one wins
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prerendering worked. View-source showed real content. Then I looked closer at the &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and found every page carried &lt;strong&gt;two&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;og:title&lt;/code&gt; tags: the homepage's, and its own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cause was in &lt;code&gt;index.html&lt;/code&gt;. Like most SPAs, mine had a static block of social tags so that &lt;em&gt;something&lt;/em&gt; showed up when people shared links:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight html"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;&amp;lt;!-- Open Graph / Social sharing --&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;property=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"og:type"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;content=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"website"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;property=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"og:title"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;content=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"ResumeOrbitz - Build a Resume That Gets You Hired"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;property=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"og:description"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;content=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Create a professional, ATS-friendly resume..."&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;React-helmet doesn't replace those tags. It &lt;strong&gt;adds&lt;/strong&gt; its own per-page tags further down the &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. So every prerendered page ended up with two &lt;code&gt;og:title&lt;/code&gt; tags: the generic one first, the correct one second.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Browsers don't care. Link-preview bots do, and many of them simply take the first tag they find. Left alone, every page on my site would have told LinkedIn it was the homepage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The fix:&lt;/strong&gt; strip the static OG and Twitter tags during prerendering, once Helmet's per-page tags are in place. The template keeps its defaults for the non-prerendered fallback, but every prerendered page has exactly one set of tags: its own.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;evaluate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;querySelectorAll&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;meta[name="description"]:not([data-rh]), &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;meta[property^="og:"]:not([data-rh]), &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;meta[name^="twitter:"]:not([data-rh])&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;forEach&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;remove&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;());&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Helmet marks every tag it adds with &lt;code&gt;data-rh&lt;/code&gt;, so anything without it is a static leftover. &lt;code&gt;app-shell.html&lt;/code&gt; keeps its defaults because it's copied before this runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you view-source a ResumeOrbitz page today, you can still see where the bug was. The &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;!-- Open Graph --&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;!-- Twitter Card --&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; comments are there, with nothing underneath, and the real tags sit lower down, marked &lt;code&gt;data-rh="true"&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Check your own site in 2 minutes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don't need any tools beyond a terminal:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# 1. Does the raw HTML have real content, or an empty root?&lt;/span&gt;
curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; https://yoursite.com/some-page | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-o&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'&amp;lt;h1[^&amp;gt;]*&amp;gt;[^&amp;lt;]*'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# 2. How many og:title tags does the page ship? It should be exactly 1.&lt;/span&gt;
curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; https://yoursite.com/some-page | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'property="og:title"'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then paste a URL into &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/post-inspector/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;LinkedIn's Post Inspector&lt;/a&gt;. It shows exactly what LinkedIn's bot reads, and refreshes its cached preview while it's at it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If check 2 returns &lt;code&gt;2&lt;/code&gt;, you have the same bug I had.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I'd tell past me
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're starting a content-heavy React app today, pick a framework with SSR or static generation from day one. If you're already deep into a Vite SPA like I was, a prerender script is a good middle path: one file, no rewrite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Just check your &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; after you do it. The page can look perfect in the browser while every share bot reads the wrong tags.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Disclosure: I'm the founder of &lt;a href="https://resumeorbitz.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ResumeOrbitz&lt;/a&gt;, the site used as the example here.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Over to you: did you go SSR or prerender for your React app? And did the OG tags bite you too?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript is one of the most important programming languages used in modern &lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;web development&lt;/a&gt;. It plays a crucial role in making websites interactive, dynamic, and user-friendly. While HTML provides the structure of a webpage and CSS handles its design and appearance, JavaScript adds functionality and enables users to interact with different website elements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fy6ki2aab4ivbru3ggz0y.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fy6ki2aab4ivbru3ggz0y.jpg" alt=" " width="799" height="454"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://dev.tourl"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
From displaying interactive menus and animations to developing complex web applications, JavaScript has become an essential technology for creating modern digital experiences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Making Websites Interactive&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the primary roles of JavaScript is to make websites interactive. It allows developers to create elements that respond to user actions, such as clicking buttons, submitting forms, scrolling pages, and selecting options.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Displaying pop-up messages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Creating dropdown menus.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Validating forms before submission.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Showing and hiding website content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These features improve user engagement and make websites easier to navigate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dynamic Content Updates&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript allows developers to update website content without reloading the entire webpage. Using the Document Object Model (DOM), developers can modify HTML elements, change text, update images, and adjust styles dynamically.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an e-commerce website can instantly update the shopping cart quantity when a customer adds a product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Form Validation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript helps validate user input before sending information to the server. It can check email formats, passwords, phone numbers, and mandatory fields.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This reduces unnecessary server requests and helps users correct errors immediately. However, server-side validation is also necessary for security and data accuracy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Asynchronous Communication&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript supports asynchronous communication through technologies such as Fetch API and AJAX. These technologies allow websites to exchange data with servers without refreshing the entire page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Loading new posts on social media.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Displaying live search suggestions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Updating weather information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fetching product details from an online store.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This makes websites faster and provides a smoother browsing experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Developing Modern Web Applications&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript is widely used to develop complex web applications. Popular frameworks and libraries such as React.js, Angular, and Vue.js help developers build scalable, reusable, and efficient user interfaces.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These technologies are commonly used in:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;E-commerce platforms.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Social networking websites.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Online banking applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Content management systems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Business dashboards.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Server-Side Development&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript is not limited to frontend development. With Node.js, developers can also use JavaScript on the server side.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It helps build APIs, handle database operations, manage authentication, and process application requests. This allows developers to use a single programming language across both frontend and backend development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Improving User Experience&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;JavaScript&lt;/a&gt; enables features such as animations, interactive maps, sliders, real-time notifications, and multimedia controls. These functionalities make websites more engaging and improve the overall user experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI is weirdly confident about React hooks. Ask for “extract the data fetching into a custom hook” and you’ll get something that &lt;em&gt;looks&lt;/em&gt; idiomatic: &lt;code&gt;useSomething&lt;/code&gt;, a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;, a couple of &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;s, maybe a &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; for good measure. Types pass. The demo works once. Then you merge and discover the effect fires twice on every keystroke, or the cleanup never ran, or the hook is just a wrapper around three lines that didn’t need a name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I treat AI-suggested hooks like AI-suggested refactors: useful drafts, not mergeable by default. Here’s the smell checklist I run before I accept the branch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Smell 1: &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; dependency lies
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The model loves this pattern:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetchUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;setUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// "run once on mount"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Except &lt;code&gt;userId&lt;/code&gt; changed three routes ago, and the effect still holds the first id. Or the opposite lie:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;analytics&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;track&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;view&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// object identity thrash&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Every parent re-render invents a new &lt;code&gt;item&lt;/code&gt; object, so “track once” becomes “track forever.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; for every &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;, I ask “what should actually retrigger this?” If the answer is “once,” I want an explicit id/primitive in the deps, or an abortable fetch keyed by that id — not an empty array with a closed-over value. If the answer is “when X changes,” I want &lt;code&gt;X&lt;/code&gt; to be a stable primitive or a memoized value I trust, not a fresh object from JSX.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Smell 2: unstable callbacks as dependencies
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Classic AI move: wrap a handler in &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt;, then put that callback in another hook’s deps… while the callback itself depends on props that change every render.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onSave&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;save&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;formState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;formState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// formState changes constantly&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;keydown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onSave&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;removeEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;keydown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onSave&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;onSave&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// rebinds every keystroke of formState&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; didn’t stabilize anything. It just moved the thrash one layer down.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; if a callback is only a dep because another effect needs it, I prefer reading the latest value from a ref, or narrowing the callback’s deps to the primitives it actually needs. “Add &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt;” is not a fix by itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Smell 3: derived state stuffed into &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The agent sees &lt;code&gt;fullName&lt;/code&gt; used in three places and invents:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fullName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setFullName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setFullName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;last&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;last&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That’s derived data. It doesn’t need state. It needs:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fullName&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;last&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Or a &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; if the derivation is actually expensive (it almost never is for string concat).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; any &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; + &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; pair that only syncs from other props/state is guilty until proven otherwise. AI does this a lot because “state” feels like the React vocabulary for “a value I can name.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Smell 4: missing cleanup (or fake cleanup)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fetch-in-effect without abort. Subscriptions without unsubscribe. &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt; without clear. The demo looks fine because you never navigate away mid-request.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cancelled&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;load&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cancelled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cancelled&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That’s the minimum. Better: &lt;code&gt;AbortController&lt;/code&gt; so the network work actually stops. AI often ships the happy path only.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; every effect that starts async work, timers, or listeners must have a cleanup that matches. I also watch for “cleanup” that only sets a flag but still lets expensive work continue — fine for setState races, not fine for upload loops.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Smell 5: over-abstraction into &lt;code&gt;useX&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not every three lines deserve a hook.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useIsOpen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;initial&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setOpen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;initial&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setOpen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setOpen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Cute. Also a digression in code review. Custom hooks earn their name when they encapsulate a &lt;strong&gt;non-obvious contract&lt;/strong&gt;: syncing with a store, coordinating multiple effects, sharing behavior across distant components. “Boolean with open/close” is just state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; would I explain this hook in a PR description without sounding silly? If the hook exists only so the component file is shorter, I inline it. AI loves extracting; I love deleting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Smell 6: stale closures dressed as “helpers”
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useSearch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[])&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Looks fine until &lt;code&gt;filter&lt;/code&gt; gets passed to a child as a callback and the child memoizes it, or until someone moves &lt;code&gt;filter&lt;/code&gt; into a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;[]&lt;/code&gt;. Closures over &lt;code&gt;items&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;query&lt;/code&gt; go stale in ways that are hard to see in a generated diff.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; values computed from props/state should either be plain expressions in render, or memoized with the &lt;em&gt;right&lt;/em&gt; deps — not casually closed over inside nested functions the agent plans to “reuse.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Smell 7: “just add another &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;”
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When something re-renders too often, the model’s first instinct is memoization:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;sorted&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;byName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;sorted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;isVisible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;sorted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isVisible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;toRow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Three memos, still thrashing if &lt;code&gt;items&lt;/code&gt; is a new array every parent render. Memo is a tax you pay after you’ve fixed identity and state shape — not a perfume you spray on the smell.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; before accepting a memo, I ask what identity is unstable upstream. Fix that first. Keep &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; for real work (big lists, expensive pure transforms), not for &lt;code&gt;obj.a + obj.b&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Smell 8: hooks that hide side effects
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The scariest AI hooks look pure in the name and impure in the body:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCartTotal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Cart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;localStorage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setItem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;cart-total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lines&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;reduce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;l&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;l&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;price&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Callers think they’re reading a number. They’re also writing storage. Side effects buried in “getter” hooks are how you get “why did navigating to settings clear my draft?” bugs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; naming. &lt;code&gt;useX&lt;/code&gt; that returns data should not silently write, fetch, or mutate globals. If it does I/O, the name should say so (&lt;code&gt;usePersistCartTotal&lt;/code&gt;) and the call site should look intentional.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


My merge gate (short version)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before I merge an AI-touched hook, I want yes on all of these:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Deps match the real trigger (no empty-array lies, no object thrash)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; for values that are pure derivations&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Async / listeners / timers have matching cleanup&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The hook earns its name (shared or non-obvious contract)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No drive-by &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; pile-on&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Side effects are obvious from the name and the call site&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If two or more fail, I don’t “tweak the hook.” I ask the agent for a smaller change: inline the logic, or rewrite the effect with an explicit contract in the prompt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Prompt that produces fewer smelly hooks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I &lt;em&gt;do&lt;/em&gt; want a custom hook from the model, I fence it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Extract a hook only if the behavior is reused or the effect graph is non-trivial.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Prefer derived values over syncing state in effects.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Every &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; must list real deps and a cleanup.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Do not add &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;/&lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; unless you can point to a measured or structural reason.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
No hidden I/O in hooks named like getters.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That doesn’t make the model perfect. It makes the first draft close enough that the smell checklist is a quick pass instead of a rewrite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hooks are easy to generate and expensive to debug after merge. Five minutes of smell-checking beats an afternoon of “why does this fire twice in Strict Mode only on the settings page.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un estado de carga parece una pieza pequeña de la interfaz. Sin embargo, decide si una persona entiende lo que ocurre o pulsa el botón varias veces. En un formulario de registro, por ejemplo, la espera puede incluir una petición, una validación y la llegada de un email. Si cada paso cambia el layout, el producto se siente lento aunque la red no sea el principal problema.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mi regla práctica es esta: una carga debe conservar el espacio, explicar el siguiente paso y tener una salida clara si algo falla. No hace falta una animación espectacular. Hace falta que el diseño, React y la accesibilidad cuenten la misma historia.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


El problema no es esperar, es no entender la espera
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un patrón bastante común muestra un spinner después de pulsar “Continuar”. El botón desaparece, aparece un texto debajo y, unos milisegundos después, todo vuelve a su sitio. Ese salto visual aumenta la sensación de inestabilidad y puede hacer que alguien pulse otra vez.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;También hay otro fallo: usar el mismo estado para “estamos comprobando”, “se envió el email” y “no pudimos completar la acción”. Son situaciones diferentes. El usuario necesita saber si debe esperar, revisar su bandeja o corregir un dato.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En flujos de QA aparecen búsquedas como &lt;code&gt;tempmailso&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;temp mail so&lt;/code&gt; y hasta el typo &lt;code&gt;tempail&lt;/code&gt;. No son estados de la interfaz, claro, pero sirven para recordar que la parte de email tiene varios pasos. El frontend no debería presentar todos como un simple “cargando”.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Un estado de carga que conserva el layout
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Primero defino un contrato pequeño para el componente:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;idle&lt;/code&gt;: el formulario acepta interacción.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;loading&lt;/code&gt;: la petición está en curso y la acción se puede bloquear.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;success&lt;/code&gt;: la operación terminó y se explica qué hacer ahora.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;error&lt;/code&gt;: hay un mensaje útil y una acción para intentarlo de nuevo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El espacio reservado es importante. En vez de insertar un aviso que empuja el formulario, dejo un contenedor con una altura mínima. Así, el botón y los campos no bailan cuando cambia el mensaje. Es una decisión de CSS, pero mejora el rendimiento percibido sin fingir que la operación terminó antes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un componente sencillo puede ser suficiente:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;loading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;success&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;StatusMessageProps&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;StatusMessage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;StatusMessageProps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"status-space"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-hidden&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"true"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`status status--&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-live&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;assertive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;polite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;El estado &lt;code&gt;idle&lt;/code&gt; conserva el hueco, mientras que los demás estados ofrecen información. En una aplicación real, evitaría que &lt;code&gt;message&lt;/code&gt; sea una cadena genérica como “Error”. El texto debería decir qué pasó y cuál es la siguiente acción, por ejemplo: “No recibimos respuesta. Intenta de nuevo en unos segundos”.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


React y CSS para feedback accesible
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El atributo &lt;code&gt;aria-busy&lt;/code&gt; puede indicar que una región está siendo actualizada. No lo pondría en toda la página: prefiero aplicarlo al bloque que realmente cambia.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-busy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;loading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-labelledby&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"signup-title"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"signup-title"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Crear cuenta&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;EmailForm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;disabled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;loading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusMessage&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Para el estilo, una reserva mínima y una animación discreta suelen bastar:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.status-space&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="py"&gt;min-block-size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;2.75rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="py"&gt;min-block-size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;2.75rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="py"&gt;margin-block&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0.75rem&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;padding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0.65rem&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0.8rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;border-radius&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0.4rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.status--loading&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;#394150&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;background&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;#f1f4f8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reduce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;animation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;none&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Hay un detalle que se olvida facil: el contraste y el foco. Un color amarillo claro puede verse “amable” en el diseño, pero no comunica nada si el texto se pierde. También conviene revisar el flujo con teclado y con un lector de pantalla; el spinner por si solo no explica el progreso.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cuando el proceso depende de varios eventos, una evidencia separada ayuda al equipo. Por ejemplo, puedes guardar un &lt;a href="https://dev.to/silviutech/agentes-llm-un-recibo-para-emails-de-prueba-3l5i"&gt;recibo de email para pruebas automatizadas&lt;/a&gt; y conectar ese resultado con el estado visible. Si el backend necesita aislamiento entre entornos, revisa también el patrón de &lt;a href="https://dev.to/oliviachen7/fastapi-emails-aislados-por-branch-3i51-temp-slug-9747599?preview=15d0f24ab23156ff7add433341db5b88816d63c95c3ecff5b0582256604f6e1410fd3181238aaa64757da0be78c0c8b8b797801df629e31b80e4e55a"&gt;emails aislados por rama de desarrollo&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Medir la experiencia sin inventar precisión
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No mediría solo el tiempo hasta que desaparece el spinner. Una vista más util es registrar:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;cuánto tarda en aparecer el primer feedback;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;cuánto tiempo permanece cada estado;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;cuántas veces se repite la acción;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;cuántos errores terminan en reintento o abandono.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Estas señales ayudan a separar un problema de backend de uno de comunicación. Si la respuesta llega rapido pero muchas personas repiten el clic, quizá el botón no muestra suficiente confirmación. Si el estado de error aparece sin una acción clara, el tiempo de red no cuenta toda la historia.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Checklist antes de publicar
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El layout conserva el espacio durante la carga?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El botón tiene foco y queda deshabilitado solo cuando corresponde?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El mensaje diferencia espera, éxito y error?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿Un lector de pantalla recibe el cambio sin demasiados anuncios?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿La interfaz funciona con teclado y con &lt;code&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El test cubre una respuesta lenta y un reintento?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Q&amp;amp;A rápido
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


¿Siempre necesito un skeleton?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. Un skeleton funciona cuando conoces la forma del contenido que llegará. Para una acción breve, un mensaje estable y un botón con estado claro suele ser más honesto.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


¿Debo mostrar porcentaje de progreso?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Solo si puedes calcularlo de forma fiable. Un porcentaje inventado crea más dudas. Para una petición de email, “Comprobando el envío” puede ser mejor que avanzar de 10% a 90% sin evidencia.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


¿Qué aporta CSS al rendimiento?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No reduce por sí solo el tiempo del servidor, pero puede evitar saltos, repintados innecesarios y confusión. Esa combinación hace que la interfaz parezca más rapida y, sobre todo, sea más entendible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI code generators write broken accessibility by default, but not because LLMs are incapable of writing semantic HTML. The issue is that an LLM does not know when it has produced inaccessible code, and most developers do not verify what it outputs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built 14 production Next.js applications with Claude Code over the last six months. Every single one passes WCAG 2.1 Level AA across roughly 350 unique screens, using Next.js 16, Tailwind CSS 4, and shadcn/ui. Every app passes axe-core with zero critical or serious violations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This worked because I set up a verification workflow that catches Claude's mistakes the same way you would catch a junior engineer's mistakes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The myth about AI and accessibility
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The common claim on developer forums is that AI code generators cannot produce accessible code because LLMs hallucinate ARIA attributes and ignore semantic HTML.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is half true. LLMs do hallucinate ARIA attributes. Claude has generated &lt;code&gt;role="container"&lt;/code&gt; (which does not exist) and &lt;code&gt;aria-pressed="yes"&lt;/code&gt; (which should be &lt;code&gt;"true"&lt;/code&gt;) right in front of me. I have watched it output buttons as divs, form inputs without labels, and contrast ratios failing at 2.1:1.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The flaw in the argument is the conclusion. AI generates broken code, but so do humans. Experienced developers catch their own mistakes because they review their work against a checklist and run automated tooling. Most teams using AI skip both.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The solution is not to stop using AI. It is to enforce a repeatable review discipline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 1: Start with an accessibility-first system prompt
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude Code follows the priorities defined in your system prompt. If you omit accessibility requirements, the model optimizes for visual layout and generation speed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I add this block to &lt;code&gt;CLAUDE.md&lt;/code&gt; in every project before asking Claude to write code:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight markdown"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gh"&gt;# Accessibility requirements (NON-NEGOTIABLE)&lt;/span&gt;

Every component you write must pass WCAG 2.1 AA. Specifically:
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
1.&lt;/span&gt; Use semantic HTML before reaching for ARIA. &lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;button&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; not &lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onclick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;.
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;nav&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; not &lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;role=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"navigation"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;. &lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;main&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; not &lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"main"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
2.&lt;/span&gt; Every interactive element must work with keyboard alone. Tab to focus,
Enter/Space to activate, Esc to close. Test by mentally walking through
the tab order.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
3.&lt;/span&gt; Every form input must have an associated &lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;label&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;. Placeholders are
not labels.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
4.&lt;/span&gt; Color contrast: 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text and UI
components. Use the design tokens from globals.css; do not invent
new colors.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
5.&lt;/span&gt; Focus indicators must be visible against every background the
component can sit on. Ring-2 with offset, never ring-1 with opacity.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
6.&lt;/span&gt; Status changes must be announced to screen readers via aria-live
or role="status". Toasts use sonner with role="status".
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
7.&lt;/span&gt; Never use color alone to convey meaning. Always pair color with
icon or text.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
8.&lt;/span&gt; Modals trap focus, return focus to trigger on close, close on Esc.
Use shadcn Dialog (built on Radix), not custom implementations.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
9.&lt;/span&gt; Touch targets are at least 44x44px on mobile. Use min-h-11 on tap
targets that look smaller.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
10.&lt;/span&gt; Test the following before marking anything complete:
&lt;span class="p"&gt;    -&lt;/span&gt; Tab through every interactive element
&lt;span class="p"&gt;    -&lt;/span&gt; Resize browser to 320px wide (no horizontal scroll)
&lt;span class="p"&gt;    -&lt;/span&gt; Toggle dark mode (contrast still passes)
&lt;span class="p"&gt;    -&lt;/span&gt; Run the page through axe DevTools (zero criticals)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Claude still makes mistakes with this in place, but they happen far less often, and the model fixes them faster when reminded of its own rules.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 2: Use shadcn/ui as the base instead of custom components
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;shadcn/ui wraps Radix UI primitives. Radix handles focus management, keyboard navigation, ARIA attributes, and screen reader announcements out of the box.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Whenever a screen needs a button, modal, dropdown, dialog, or popover, use the shadcn implementation. Never let Claude build a custom modal or dropdown from scratch. It will eventually drop the focus trap or miss the &lt;code&gt;aria-expanded&lt;/code&gt; attribute.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 3: Run axe-core on every screen
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After Claude generates a screen, run axe DevTools on the rendered page. It takes a few minutes and catches roughly 60% of automated accessibility issues.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To automate this in your test suite, use &lt;code&gt;@axe-core/playwright&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@playwright/test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;AxeBuilder&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@axe-core/playwright&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;dashboard has no a11y violations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;goto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/dashboard&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;AxeBuilder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;withTags&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;wcag2a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;wcag2aa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;wcag21a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;wcag21aa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;wcag22aa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;analyze&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;violations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toEqual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([])&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;When this test fails, copy the exact violation details back into Claude Code and tell it to fix them. Claude is bad at preventing violations on the initial generation, but it is reliable at fixing them when given the exact failing rule.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 4: Run a manual keyboard test
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Static tools miss dynamic interaction bugs. A screen can pass axe-core while remaining unusable without a mouse. For every screen:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Navigate to the page.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Press Tab repeatedly through every interactive element.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Check that the tab order follows the visual reading order.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Trigger actions using only the keyboard: Enter or Space for buttons, arrow keys for menus, Esc to dismiss overlays.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Watch for elements that trap focus inappropriately or drop focus entirely.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This takes about 90 seconds per view and catches broken dialog traps or menus that fail to focus their first item on open.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 5: Run a screen reader smoke test
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Turn on VoiceOver on macOS (Cmd+F5) or NVDA on Windows and walk the main user flow. Check three things:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Meaningful labels: every interactive item announces a clear label, not just "button" or "edit text".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dynamic updates: loading spinners, form error states, and success toasts trigger announcements.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Headings: the hierarchy is logical, starting with one &lt;code&gt;h1&lt;/code&gt;, followed by &lt;code&gt;h2&lt;/code&gt; sections, without skipped levels.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 6: Test color contrast across all themes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;shadcn relies on CSS custom properties for theming. A color pair that passes contrast in light mode can easily fail in dark mode.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most frequent regression is &lt;code&gt;muted-foreground&lt;/code&gt; placed on top of &lt;code&gt;muted&lt;/code&gt; backgrounds. Check every theme permutation explicitly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Common Claude mistakes and the fix prompts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Across hundreds of generated screens, Claude repeatedly falls into the same specific traps:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Clickable divs
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude will build a card as a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;div&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; with an &lt;code&gt;onClick&lt;/code&gt; handler. It looks right visually, but screen readers and keyboards ignore it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt: "Make every clickable card a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;button&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; or wrap it in a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Link&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; from next/link, never a div with onClick."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Skipped heading levels
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude often puts an &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;h1&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; on the title and jumps immediately to &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;h3&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; for cards to match visual styling, violating WCAG 1.3.1.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt: "Heading levels must be sequential. After h1, the next level can only be h2. After h2, only h2 or h3. Never skip a level."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Placeholders instead of labels
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inputs will get &lt;code&gt;placeholder="Email address"&lt;/code&gt; with no &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;label&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; element. Once the user types, the placeholder disappears, and screen readers lose context.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt: "Every form input must have a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;label&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; element associated via &lt;code&gt;htmlFor&lt;/code&gt;. Use the shadcn &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Label&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; component. The placeholder is for example text only, never as the label."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. Custom modals without focus traps
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If Claude writes a modal without Radix primitives, tabbing escapes the overlay into background elements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt: "Always use the shadcn &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Dialog&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; component for modals. Never build a custom modal. Radix Dialog handles focus trap, focus return, and Esc-to-close correctly."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


5. Silent asynchronous updates
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude generates a "Saving..." text label on submit, but omits an announcement region.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt: "Status messages must be in a region with &lt;code&gt;role='status'&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;aria-live='polite'&lt;/code&gt; so screen readers announce them. For toast notifications, use sonner which handles this automatically."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


6. Relying purely on color
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inputs fail with a red border alone, without an error message or icon, which violates WCAG 1.4.1.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt: "Status indicators must combine color, icon, and text. Failed inputs need a red border AND an error message AND an aria-invalid attribute. Successful submits need a green icon AND text AND a status announcement."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


7. Hallucinated ARIA
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude writes attributes that do not exist in the WAI-ARIA specification, such as &lt;code&gt;aria-tooltip&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;aria-hidden="yes"&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt: "Pay especially close attention to attribute names and boolean values. Ensure every ARIA attribute matches the W3C specification exactly."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What cannot be delegated to Claude
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude Code handled the bulk of the initial markup, but specific tasks required manual implementation:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Recharts SVGs: Chart graphics have no accessible fallback out of the box. You must write visually hidden &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;table&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; summaries alongside each chart so screen readers can consume the underlying dataset.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dynamic announcement copy: Specific announcements like "Filter applied, showing 47 results" get missed by LLMs unless explicitly requested.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Physical screen reader listening: No automated tool replaces testing an interface with VoiceOver or NVDA to verify the spoken reading order.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a shorter version of a guide I wrote at Frontkit Studio. The &lt;a href="https://thefrontkit.com/blogs/how-i-built-14-accessible-nextjs-apps-with-claude-code?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;full post&lt;/a&gt; has the complete walkthrough.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
